{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FaT6utZzCQd4dCMdz9uEYk","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Original framing fully intact. Story: non-paraphrastic chains\n2\t  exhibit monotonic distributional divergence (H₂ confirmed), detectable via reference-model\n3\t  entropy features that capture drift beyond surface vocabulary impoverishment. Headline:\n4\t  \"distributional fingerprints enable risk-stratified data curation,\" with clean ablation\n5\t  separating signal from shortcut. All three claims stated at full strength. AAAI\n6\t  submission with no scope reduction.\n7\t\n8\t  '\n9\timagined_experiments:\n10\t- name: feature_ablation\n11\t  plan_id: plan_001\n12\t  description: 'Ablation on existing MVP data (4609 records, Pythia-2.8B generator,\n13\t    Pythia-410M reference, Wikipedia seeds). Three conditions: (A) reference-only\n14\t    features (entropy_mean, entropy_std, entropy_min, entropy_max, tail_decay), (B)\n15\t    surface-only features (ttr, hapax_ratio), (C) full 7-feature set. Same XGBoost\n16\t    classifier, 5-fold stratified CV, 3-class (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+).\n17\t\n18\t    '\n19\t  expected_results:\n20\t    ref_only_macro_f1: 0.79\n21\t    ref_only_cv_std: 0.018\n22\t    ref_only_gen0_f1: 0.91\n23\t    ref_only_gen1_f1: 0.71\n24\t    ref_only_gen2plus_f1: 0.74\n25\t    surface_only_macro_f1: 0.63\n26\t    surface_only_cv_std: 0.022\n27\t    surface_only_gen0_f1: 0.82\n28\t    surface_only_gen1_f1: 0.52\n29\t    surface_only_gen2plus_f1: 0.55\n30\t    full_macro_f1: 0.87\n31\t    full_cv_std: 0.012\n32\t    full_gen0_f1: 0.96\n33\t    full_gen1_f1: 0.81\n34\t    full_gen2plus_f1: 0.83\n35\t    ref_minus_surface_delta: 0.16\n36\t    mcnemar_p_ref_vs_surface: 3.0e-05\n37\t    permutation_importance_entropy_mean: 0.28\n38\t    permutation_importance_entropy_std: 0.19\n39\t    permutation_importance_hapax_ratio: 0.14\n40\t    permutation_importance_ttr: 0.09\n41\t  result_rationale: 'MVP feature importance (hapax_ratio=0.30) was inflated by XGBoost''s\n42\t    split-gain metric, which overweights high-cardinality continuous features with\n43\t    many useful split points. When surface features are ablated, the 5 reference-model\n44\t    features retain macro-F1=0.79 because they directly measure the generator''s deviation\n45\t    from the reference model''s next-token distribution — a signal that accumulates\n46\t    monotonically with depth as the generator''s output drifts further from human\n47\t    text. Entropy_mean captures the average surprise under the reference model (increases\n48\t    with depth as generated text occupies lower-probability regions), while entropy_std\n49\t    captures the increasing variance of surprise (deeper text alternates between familiar\n50\t    patterns and novel drift). Surface features alone (F1=0.63) capture only vocabulary\n51\t    impoverishment — TTR and hapax_ratio decline with depth as generators recycle\n52\t    high-frequency tokens — but this is a weaker, noisier proxy. The 0.16 delta and\n53\t    significant McNemar test confirm reference features carry independent, dominant\n54\t    signal. The full set achieves F1=0.87 (up from MVP''s 0.84) because retuned hyperparameters\n55\t    on the same data with proper ablation awareness extract slightly more complementary\n56\t    value. Permutation importance (which avoids split-gain bias) reranks entropy_mean\n57\t    as the top feature.\n58\t\n59\t    '\n60\t  tables:\n61\t  - name: 'Table 1: Feature Ablation Results (3-class depth classification)'\n62\t    columns:\n63\t    - Feature Set\n64\t    - Features\n65\t    - Macro-F1\n66\t    - gen-0 F1\n67\t    - gen-1 F1\n68\t    - gen-2+ F1\n69\t    - CV std\n70\t    rows:\n71\t    - - Reference-only\n72\t      - entropy_{mean,std,min,max}, tail_decay\n73\t      - 0.79\n74\t      - 0.91\n75\t      - 0.71\n76\t      - 0.74\n77\t      - 0.018\n78\t    - - Surface-only\n79\t      - ttr, hapax_ratio\n80\t      - 0.63\n81\t      - 0.82\n82\t      - 0.52\n83\t      - 0.55\n84\t      - 0.022\n85\t    - - Full\n86\t      - All 7 features\n87\t      - 0.87\n88\t      - 0.96\n89\t      - 0.81\n90\t      - 0.83\n91\t      - 0.012\n92\t    - - Δ (ref − surface)\n93\t      - —\n94\t      - '+0.16'\n95\t      - '+0.09'\n96\t      - '+0.19'\n97\t      - '+0.19'\n98\t      - —\n99\t  - name: 'Table 2: Permutation Importance (Full model, 5-fold mean)'\n100\t    columns:\n101\t    - Feature\n102\t    - Permutation Importance\n103\t    - XGBoost Gain (MVP)\n104\t    rows:\n105\t    - - entropy_mean\n106\t      - 0.28\n107\t      - 0.22\n108\t    - - entropy_std\n109\t      - 0.19\n110\t      - 0.13\n111\t    - - hapax_ratio\n112\t      - 0.14\n113\t      - 0.3\n114\t    - - tail_decay\n115\t      - 0.13\n116\t      - 0.06\n117\t    - - ttr\n118\t      - 0.09\n119\t      - 0.17\n120\t    - - entropy_max\n121\t      - 0.09\n122\t      - 0.07\n123\t    - - entropy_min\n124\t      - 0.08\n125\t      - 0.06\n126\t  figures_description:\n127\t  - 'Fig 1a: Grouped bar chart — Macro-F1 for ref-only / surface-only / full, with\n128\t    95% CI error bars from CV. Clear visual gap between ref-only and surface-only.'\n129\t  - 'Fig 1b: Per-class F1 heatmap (3 feature sets × 3 classes). Shows that the surface→ref\n130\t    gap is largest for gen-1 and gen-2+ (the harder classes), confirming reference\n131\t    features add discriminability precisely where surface shortcuts fail.'\n132\t  - 'Fig 1c: Permutation importance bar chart (full model) vs XGBoost gain bar chart\n133\t    (MVP). Side-by-side comparison reveals hapax_ratio''s gain was inflated; entropy_mean\n134\t    is the true top feature under permutation.'\n135\t  analysis: 'The ablation cleanly validates Claim 2. The ref-only advantage is strongest\n136\t    on gen-1 (Δ=+0.19) and gen-2+ (Δ=+0.19), the classes where surface features struggle\n137\t    most — TTR/hapax can separate human text from any synthetic text but cannot distinguish\n138\t    depth levels within synthetic text, because vocabulary impoverishment saturates\n139\t    quickly (mostly between gen-0→gen-1). Reference-model entropy features, by contrast,\n140\t    capture cumulative distributional drift that continues to grow between gen-1 and\n141\t    gen-2+. The reranking of feature importance under permutation (entropy_mean rises\n142\t    to 0.28, hapax_ratio drops to 0.14) directly addresses RC1 (\"Is this just vocabulary\n143\t    impoverishment?\") — it is not. The full model''s improvement to 0.87 suggests\n144\t    the two feature families capture partially orthogonal information: surface features\n145\t    provide a cheap gen-0 vs rest shortcut, while reference features provide the finer\n146\t    gen-1 vs gen-2+ signal.\n147\t\n148\t    '\n149\t- name: cross_generator_evaluation\n150\t  plan_id: plan_002\n151\t  description: 'Generate 25K chains: 1000 Wikipedia seeds × 5 generators (Pythia-2.8B,\n152\t    OLMo-2-1B, Qwen3.5-2B, Qwen3.5-0.8B, Pythia-1B) × 5 depths. Feature extraction\n153\t    with Pythia-410M reference model. Leave-one-generator-out CV (train on 4 generators,\n154\t    test on held-out generator). Also report per-generator in-distribution results\n155\t    and per-class breakdowns.\n156\t\n157\t    '\n158\t  expected_results:\n159\t    leave_one_out_macro_f1_mean: 0.78\n160\t    leave_one_out_macro_f1_std: 0.03\n161\t    leave_one_out_gen1_f1_mean: 0.73\n162\t    leave_one_out_gen2plus_f1_mean: 0.75\n163\t    leave_one_out_gen0_f1_mean: 0.88\n164\t    in_distribution_5fold_macro_f1: 0.86\n165\t    per_generator_held_out:\n166\t      pythia_2_8b: 0.81\n167\t      olmo_2_1b: 0.77\n168\t      qwen3_5_2b: 0.76\n169\t      qwen3_5_0_8b: 0.74\n170\t      pythia_1b: 0.82\n171\t    total_chains_generated: 24200\n172\t    attrition_rate_pct: 3.2\n173\t    generation_gpu_hours: 18.5\n174\t  result_rationale: 'Cross-generator generalization holds (mean leave-one-out F1=0.78)\n175\t    because distributional drift is measured from the reference model''s perspective,\n176\t    not the generator''s. All generators produce text that progressively deviates\n177\t    from human text distribution as measured by Pythia-410M; the drift trajectory\n178\t    differs in magnitude but shares the same directional pattern (entropy increases,\n179\t    tail probability mass shifts). Pythia-family generators (2.8B, 1B) transfer best\n180\t    to each other (F1=0.81–0.82) due to shared pretraining distribution. Qwen3.5-0.8B\n181\t    is the hardest held-out case (F1=0.74) because its smaller capacity produces qualitatively\n182\t    different text that drifts faster at early depths — the classifier trained on\n183\t    larger models underestimates Qwen-0.8B''s depth. The gen-1 vs gen-2+ boundary\n184\t    remains the bottleneck (gen-1 F1=0.73) because gen-1 text from some generators\n185\t    overlaps with gen-2+ text from others in feature space. In-distribution pooled\n186\t    performance (0.86) is close to MVP (0.84) despite mixing generators, confirming\n187\t    that generator diversity adds training signal rather than noise. Attrition (~3.2%)\n188\t    is lower than MVP (3.7%) due to improved generation pipeline with retry logic.\n189\t\n190\t    '\n191\t  tables:\n192\t  - name: 'Table 3: Leave-One-Generator-Out Cross-Validation'\n193\t    columns:\n194\t    - Held-out Generator\n195\t    - Macro-F1\n196\t    - gen-0 F1\n197\t    - gen-1 F1\n198\t    - gen-2+ F1\n199\t    rows:\n200\t    - - Pythia-2.8B\n201\t      - 0.81\n202\t      - 0.91\n203\t      - 0.75\n204\t      - 0.77\n205\t    - - OLMo-2-1B\n206\t      - 0.77\n207\t      - 0.87\n208\t      - 0.72\n209\t      - 0.73\n210\t    - - Qwen3.5-2B\n211\t      - 0.76\n212\t      - 0.86\n213\t      - 0.71\n214\t      - 0.72\n215\t    - - Qwen3.5-0.8B\n216\t      - 0.74\n217\t      - 0.84\n218\t      - 0.68\n219\t      - 0.7\n220\t    - - Pythia-1B\n221\t      - 0.82\n222\t      - 0.92\n223\t      - 0.76\n224\t      - 0.78\n225\t    - - Mean ± std\n226\t      - 0.78 ± 0.03\n227\t      - 0.88 ± 0.03\n228\t      - 0.73 ± 0.03\n229\t      - 0.75 ± 0.03\n230\t  - name: 'Table 4: Per-Generator In-Distribution 5-Fold CV'\n231\t    columns:\n232\t    - Generator\n233\t    - Macro-F1\n234\t    - CV std\n235\t    rows:\n236\t    - - Pythia-2.8B\n237\t      - 0.85\n238\t      - 0.014\n239\t    - - OLMo-2-1B\n240\t      - 0.84\n241\t      - 0.016\n242\t    - - Qwen3.5-2B\n243\t      - 0.86\n244\t      - 0.013\n245\t    - - Qwen3.5-0.8B\n246\t      - 0.83\n247\t      - 0.019\n248\t    - - Pythia-1B\n249\t      - 0.84\n250\t      - 0.015\n251\t    - - Pooled (all 5)\n252\t      - 0.86\n253\t      - 0.011\n254\t  figures_description:\n255\t  - 'Fig 2a: Leave-one-out macro-F1 bar chart with error bars, generators ordered\n256\t    by held-out performance. Horizontal dashed line at 0.78 (mean). Shows Pythia-family\n257\t    clusters above mean, Qwen-0.8B below.'\n258\t  - 'Fig 2b: Confusion matrix heatmaps (5 panels, one per held-out generator). Highlights\n259\t    that gen-1↔gen-2+ confusion is the primary failure mode across all generators.'\n260\t  - 'Fig 2c: t-SNE of reference-model features colored by depth, faceted by generator.\n261\t    Shows that depth clusters are geometrically similar across generators despite\n262\t    generator-specific offsets in feature space.'\n263\t  analysis: 'The 0.78 mean leave-one-out F1 with narrow std (0.03) strongly supports\n264\t    Claim 1''s cross-generator generalization. The pattern is consistent: gen-0 is\n265\t    always well-separated (F1≥0.84), while gen-1 and gen-2+ form a harder boundary.\n266\t    Pythia-family transfer is strongest, suggesting some same-family distributional\n267\t    similarity, but even cross-family transfer (Pythia→Qwen) maintains F1≥0.74. This\n268\t    confirms the distributional fingerprint is a property of the generation process,\n269\t    not a generator-specific artifact. The pooled in-distribution result (0.86) exceeding\n270\t    any single-generator result suggests that multi-generator training improves robustness.\n271\t    For the paper, this table is the centerpiece of Claim 1 and directly addresses\n272\t    RC2.\n273\t\n274\t    '\n275\t- name: cross_family_reference_model\n276\t  plan_id: plan_003\n277\t  description: 'Re-extract features using non-Pythia reference models (OLMo-2-1B,\n278\t    Qwen3.5-0.8B) on the plan_002 dataset (25K chains). Compare classification performance\n279\t    across 3 reference model families. Tests whether the fingerprint is reference-model-agnostic.\n280\t\n281\t    '\n282\t  expected_results:\n283\t    pythia_410m_ref_macro_f1: 0.86\n284\t    olmo_2_1b_ref_macro_f1: 0.76\n285\t    qwen3_5_0_8b_ref_macro_f1: 0.74\n286\t    ensemble_3refs_macro_f1: 0.88\n287\t    cross_ref_leave_one_out_macro_f1: 0.75\n288\t    feature_correlation_pythia_olmo_entropy_mean: 0.82\n289\t    feature_correlation_pythia_qwen_entropy_mean: 0.78\n290\t  result_rationale: 'All three reference models detect depth signal (F1≥0.74) because\n291\t    any well-trained LM assigns progressively lower probability to deeper-generation\n292\t    text — the drift is a property of the text, not the measuring instrument. Pythia-410M\n293\t    performs best (0.86) because it was the reference model used during pipeline development\n294\t    and its capacity is calibrated for sensitivity to distributional shifts (small\n295\t    enough to be \"surprised\" by drift, large enough to model human text well). OLMo-2-1B\n296\t    (0.76) and Qwen3.5-0.8B (0.74) are slightly weaker because their different pretraining\n297\t    distributions create different baselines for \"expected\" text, adding noise to\n298\t    depth features. High cross-reference entropy_mean correlations (0.78–0.82) confirm\n299\t    that all three models agree on the direction of drift even if magnitudes differ.\n300\t    The 3-reference ensemble (0.88) exceeds any single reference, indicating that\n301\t    diverse reference models capture complementary aspects of distributional drift.\n302\t    This decisively addresses RC2 — the signal is not a Pythia-specific artifact.\n303\t\n304\t    '\n305\t  tables:\n306\t  - name: 'Table 5: Reference Model Comparison (pooled 5-generator data)'\n307\t    columns:\n308\t    - Reference Model\n309\t    - Family\n310\t    - Params\n311\t    - Macro-F1\n312\t    - gen-0 F1\n313\t    - gen-1 F1\n314\t    - gen-2+ F1\n315\t    rows:\n316\t    - - Pythia-410M\n317\t      - Pythia\n318\t      - 410M\n319\t      - 0.86\n320\t      - 0.95\n321\t      - 0.8\n322\t      - 0.82\n323\t    - - OLMo-2-1B\n324\t      - OLMo\n325\t      - 1B\n326\t      - 0.76\n327\t      - 0.88\n328\t      - 0.69\n329\t      - 0.72\n330\t    - - Qwen3.5-0.8B\n331\t      - Qwen\n332\t      - 0.8B\n333\t      - 0.74\n334\t      - 0.86\n335\t      - 0.66\n336\t      - 0.7\n337\t    - - Ensemble (3 refs)\n338\t      - Mixed\n339\t      - —\n340\t      - 0.88\n341\t      - 0.96\n342\t      - 0.83\n343\t      - 0.85\n344\t  - name: 'Table 6: Pairwise Feature Correlation (Spearman ρ) Across Reference Models'\n345\t    columns:\n346\t    - Feature\n347\t    - Pythia↔OLMo\n348\t    - Pythia↔Qwen\n349\t    - OLMo↔Qwen\n350\t    rows:\n351\t    - - entropy_mean\n352\t      - 0.82\n353\t      - 0.78\n354\t      - 0.8\n355\t    - - entropy_std\n356\t      - 0.79\n357\t      - 0.74\n358\t      - 0.76\n359\t    - - tail_decay\n360\t      - 0.71\n361\t      - 0.66\n362\t      - 0.69\n363\t  figures_description:\n364\t  - 'Fig 3a: Parallel coordinates plot showing entropy_mean distributions by depth,\n365\t    one panel per reference model. Monotonic increase with depth visible in all three,\n366\t    confirming H₂ across reference architectures.'\n367\t  - 'Fig 3b: Scatter matrix of entropy_mean values across reference model pairs, colored\n368\t    by depth. Strong positive correlation with depth-dependent clustering confirms\n369\t    cross-reference consistency.'\n370\t  analysis: 'The cross-family validation eliminates the concern that Pythia-410M has\n371\t    some idiosyncratic sensitivity to synthetic text. All three reference architectures\n372\t    detect monotonic distributional drift. The ensemble''s improvement to 0.88 provides\n373\t    an optional \"best-effort\" configuration for practitioners, though the single-reference\n374\t    Pythia-410M result (0.86) is sufficient for the paper''s claims. The correlation\n375\t    analysis (Table 6) is particularly informative: high but imperfect correlations\n376\t    indicate that reference models measure the same underlying phenomenon through\n377\t    slightly different lenses, explaining why ensembling helps. For the paper, this\n378\t    experiment transforms a potential weakness (Pythia-only evaluation) into a strength\n379\t    (reference-model-agnostic signal).\n380\t\n381\t    '\n382\t- name: downstream_utility\n383\t  plan_id: plan_004\n384\t  description: 'Fine-tune Pythia-410M on depth-stratified subsets of the plan_002\n385\t    data. Measure perplexity on held-out human test set as function of training data\n386\t    depth composition. Compare depth-aware filtering (keep gen-0 + gen-1, discard\n387\t    gen-2+) vs binary baselines (FastDetectGPT threshold, LD-Score threshold) for\n388\t    preserving downstream model quality.\n389\t\n390\t    '\n391\t  expected_results:\n392\t    depth_quality_spearman_rho: 0.72\n393\t    ppl_gen0_only: 28.3\n394\t    ppl_gen1_only: 30.1\n395\t    ppl_gen2_only: 34.7\n396\t    ppl_gen3_only: 38.9\n397\t    ppl_gen4_only: 43.2\n398\t    ppl_mixed_uniform: 35.8\n399\t    ppl_depth_filtered_gen01: 29.0\n400\t    ppl_binary_fastdetectgpt: 31.8\n401\t    ppl_binary_ldscore: 32.1\n402\t    ppl_human_baseline: 27.5\n403\t    depth_filter_vs_binary_gap_pct: 5.2\n404\t    training_samples_kept_depth_filter: 0.42\n405\t    training_samples_kept_binary: 0.28\n406\t  result_rationale: 'Perplexity increases monotonically with training data depth (28.3→43.2\n407\t    from gen-0→gen-4) because deeper-generation text has compounded distributional\n408\t    drift from human text — fine-tuning on it shifts the model''s learned distribution\n409\t    away from the human test set. The Spearman ρ=0.72 between depth and perplexity\n410\t    is strong but not perfect because individual samples vary in drift magnitude (some\n411\t    gen-3 texts are less drifted than some gen-2 texts). Depth-aware filtering (keep\n412\t    gen-0+gen-1, perplexity=29.0) outperforms binary baselines (~31.8–32.1) by 5.2%\n413\t    because binary detectors discard all synthetic text including useful gen-1 content\n414\t    that is distributionally close to human text. Binary filtering retains only 28%\n415\t    of data (mostly gen-0) while depth filtering retains 42% (gen-0 + gen-1), providing\n416\t    more training data of acceptable quality. The gap between gen-0-only (28.3) and\n417\t    depth-filtered (29.0) is small, confirming that gen-1 text is nearly as good as\n418\t    human text for training — the key practical insight for data curation.\n419\t\n420\t    '\n421\t  tables:\n422\t  - name: 'Table 7: Fine-tuning Perplexity by Training Data Depth'\n423\t    columns:\n424\t    - Training Data\n425\t    - Depth Composition\n426\t    - PPL (↓)\n427\t    - Samples Used\n428\t    rows:\n429\t    - - Human-only\n430\t      - gen-0\n431\t      - 28.3\n432\t      - ~4800\n433\t    - - gen-1 only\n434\t      - gen-1\n435\t      - 30.1\n436\t      - ~4750\n437\t    - - gen-2 only\n438\t      - gen-2\n439\t      - 34.7\n440\t      - ~4500\n441\t    - - gen-3 only\n442\t      - gen-3\n443\t      - 38.9\n444\t      - ~4200\n445\t    - - gen-4 only\n446\t      - gen-4\n447\t      - 43.2\n448\t      - ~3900\n449\t    - - Uniform mix\n450\t      - gen-0–4 equal\n451\t      - 35.8\n452\t      - ~22000\n453\t    - - Depth-filtered (ours)\n454\t      - gen-0 + gen-1\n455\t      - 29.0\n456\t      - ~9500\n457\t    - - FastDetectGPT filter\n458\t      - binary threshold\n459\t      - 31.8\n460\t      - ~6300\n461\t    - - LD-Score filter\n462\t      - binary threshold\n463\t      - 32.1\n464\t      - ~6100\n465\t    - - Human test baseline\n466\t      - —\n467\t      - 27.5\n468\t      - —\n469\t  - name: 'Table 8: Depth-Aware vs Binary Filtering Comparison'\n470\t    columns:\n471\t    - Method\n472\t    - PPL\n473\t    - Data Retained (%)\n474\t    - PPL Gap to Human-Only\n475\t    rows:\n476\t    - - Depth-filtered (gen-0+1)\n477\t      - 29.0\n478\t      - 42%\n479\t      - '+0.7'\n480\t    - - FastDetectGPT binary\n481\t      - 31.8\n482\t      - 28%\n483\t      - '+3.5'\n484\t    - - LD-Score binary\n485\t      - 32.1\n486\t      - 26%\n487\t      - '+3.8'\n488\t    - - No filtering (all data)\n489\t      - 35.8\n490\t      - 100%\n491\t      - '+7.5'\n492\t  figures_description:\n493\t  - 'Fig 4a: Line plot — perplexity vs generation depth (gen-0 through gen-4). Monotonic\n494\t    increase with 95% CI bands from 3 random seeds. Confirms H₂''s downstream consequence.'\n495\t  - 'Fig 4b: Bar chart comparing filtering strategies (depth-aware, FastDetectGPT,\n496\t    LD-Score, no filter) on perplexity and data retention. Dual y-axis: bars for perplexity,\n497\t    overlaid line for % data retained.'\n498\t  - 'Fig 4c: Scatter plot — predicted depth score vs actual fine-tuning perplexity\n499\t    contribution per sample, with Spearman ρ annotation. Shows the sample-level correlation\n500\t    between depth estimate and quality impact.'\n501\t  analysis: 'This experiment validates Claim 3 and provides the practical punchline\n502\t    of the paper. The monotonic perplexity curve (Fig 4a) is the most intuitive visualization\n503\t    of H₂ — it translates distributional drift into a metric practitioners care about.\n504\t    The 5.2% gap between depth-aware and binary filtering is meaningful because it\n505\t    comes with MORE data retained (42% vs 28%), resolving the precision-recall tradeoff\n506\t    that plagues binary detection. The key insight is that gen-1 text is \"good enough\"\n507\t    for training (PPL=30.1 vs human 28.3), but gen-2+ is not (PPL≥34.7). Binary detectors\n508\t    cannot make this distinction. For AAAI reviewers, this experiment connects the\n509\t    theoretical contribution (distributional drift characterization) to a practical\n510\t    application (data curation), which is important for venue fit.\n511\t\n512\t    '\n513\t- name: prompt_type_comparison\n514\t  plan_id: plan_005\n515\t  description: 'Generate chains with 3 prompt types (continuation, elaboration, inspired_rewriting)\n516\t    using Pythia-2.8B on 1000 Wikipedia seeds × 5 depths. Compare depth signal preservation\n517\t    across prompt types. Tests whether semantic divergence from source correlates\n518\t    with depth signal.\n519\t\n520\t    '\n521\t  expected_results:\n522\t    continuation_macro_f1: 0.85\n523\t    elaboration_macro_f1: 0.8\n524\t    inspired_rewriting_macro_f1: 0.72\n525\t    continuation_entropy_drift_per_depth: 0.18\n526\t    elaboration_entropy_drift_per_depth: 0.14\n527\t    inspired_rewriting_entropy_drift_per_depth: 0.1\n528\t    cross_prompt_train_test_macro_f1: 0.74\n529\t    prompt_type_drift_rate_spearman: 0.91\n530\t  result_rationale: 'Continuation preserves the strongest depth signal (F1=0.85) because\n531\t    each generation step continues directly from the previous output, maximizing the\n532\t    accumulation of distributional drift. Elaboration (F1=0.80) injects some new semantic\n533\t    direction via the prompt instruction, which partially resets the drift trajectory\n534\t    at each step — drift still accumulates but at a slower rate. Inspired rewriting\n535\t    (F1=0.72) has the most semantic freedom: the model is instructed to produce text\n536\t    \"inspired by\" the input, allowing it to diverge from the drift trajectory and\n537\t    partially re-anchor to its pretraining distribution. The strong correlation between\n538\t    per-depth entropy drift rate and classification F1 (Spearman ρ=0.91) confirms\n539\t    the theoretical prediction: more constrained generation → faster drift accumulation\n540\t    → stronger depth signal. Cross-prompt-type transfer (F1=0.74) is moderate, indicating\n541\t    that the drift signature is partially shared across prompt types but the rate\n542\t    differences create distribution shift in feature space.\n543\t\n544\t    '\n545\t  tables:\n546\t  - name: 'Table 9: Depth Classification by Prompt Type'\n547\t    columns:\n548\t    - Prompt Type\n549\t    - Macro-F1\n550\t    - gen-0 F1\n551\t    - gen-1 F1\n552\t    - gen-2+ F1\n553\t    - Entropy Drift Rate\n554\t    rows:\n555\t    - - Continuation\n556\t      - 0.85\n557\t      - 0.95\n558\t      - 0.79\n559\t      - 0.81\n560\t      - 0.18 nats/depth\n561\t    - - Elaboration\n562\t      - 0.8\n563\t      - 0.92\n564\t      - 0.73\n565\t      - 0.75\n566\t      - 0.14 nats/depth\n567\t    - - Inspired rewriting\n568\t      - 0.72\n569\t      - 0.86\n570\t      - 0.64\n571\t      - 0.67\n572\t      - 0.10 nats/depth\n573\t  figures_description:\n574\t  - 'Fig 5a: Line plots — entropy_mean vs depth for each prompt type, showing different\n575\t    drift rates. Continuation has steepest slope.'\n576\t  - 'Fig 5b: Scatter plot — prompt type drift rate vs macro-F1, confirming linear\n577\t    relationship. Three points with regression line and ρ annotation.'\n578\t  analysis: 'This experiment decomposes the depth signal by generation modality. The\n579\t    ordering (continuation > elaboration > inspired rewriting) aligns with the theoretical\n580\t    framework: the more the generation task constrains the output to build on previous\n581\t    text, the more drift accumulates. Inspired rewriting''s lower F1 is not a failure\n582\t    — it confirms the mechanism. For the paper, this comparison strengthens the theoretical\n583\t    story by showing that depth signal is graded, not binary, and correlates with\n584\t    the degree of semantic coupling between successive generation steps.\n585\t\n586\t    '\n587\t- name: paraphrastic_comparison\n588\t  plan_id: plan_006\n589\t  description: 'Construct parallel paraphrasing chains (same 1000 seeds, Pythia-2.8B,\n590\t    5 depths) alongside continuation chains. Measure whether paraphrastic chains converge\n591\t    (per attractor theory) while continuation chains diverge (our H₂). Direct empirical\n592\t    test of H₁ vs H₂.\n593\t\n594\t    '\n595\t  expected_results:\n596\t    paraphrase_macro_f1_3class: 0.58\n597\t    paraphrase_gen0_f1: 0.81\n598\t    paraphrase_gen1_f1: 0.46\n599\t    paraphrase_gen2plus_f1: 0.47\n600\t    continuation_macro_f1_3class: 0.85\n601\t    paraphrase_entropy_drift_depth3: 0.06\n602\t    continuation_entropy_drift_depth3: 0.52\n603\t    paraphrase_cycle_detection_pct: 68.0\n604\t    divergence_test_ks_statistic: 0.41\n605\t    divergence_test_p_value: 1.0e-05\n606\t  result_rationale: 'Paraphrastic chains show weak depth signal (F1=0.58) because\n607\t    the semantic preservation constraint of paraphrasing creates attractor dynamics\n608\t    — the text oscillates around a fixed semantic point, and distributional features\n609\t    converge after ~2 steps (confirming ACL 2025 attractor cycle theory). By depth\n610\t    3, paraphrastic entropy drift has nearly plateaued (0.06 nats) while continuation\n611\t    drift continues growing (0.52 nats). The 68% cycle detection rate in paraphrastic\n612\t    chains (text at depth d is more similar to depth d-2 than d-1) directly corroborates\n613\t    the 2-period attractor cycle prediction. Continuation chains show no such periodicity\n614\t    — drift is monotonic. The KS test between depth-3 entropy distributions of paraphrastic\n615\t    vs continuation chains (D=0.41, p<0.001) provides the statistical test of H₁ vs\n616\t    H₂. This is the most theoretically clean result in the paper: H₂ (divergence)\n617\t    holds for non-paraphrastic chains, H₁ (convergence) holds for paraphrastic chains,\n618\t    exactly as predicted by the semantic constraint difference.\n619\t\n620\t    '\n621\t  tables:\n622\t  - name: 'Table 10: Paraphrastic vs Continuation Chain Comparison'\n623\t    columns:\n624\t    - Chain Type\n625\t    - Macro-F1\n626\t    - Entropy Drift @depth3\n627\t    - Cycle Detection %\n628\t    rows:\n629\t    - - Continuation\n630\t      - 0.85\n631\t      - 0.52 nats\n632\t      - < 5%\n633\t    - - Paraphrasing\n634\t      - 0.58\n635\t      - 0.06 nats\n636\t      - 68%\n637\t    - - Δ\n638\t      - '+0.27'\n639\t      - +0.46 nats\n640\t      - —\n641\t  figures_description:\n642\t  - 'Fig 6a: Dual-panel line plot — entropy_mean vs depth for continuation (diverging)\n643\t    and paraphrasing (converging). The visual contrast is the paper''s most compelling\n644\t    figure.'\n645\t  - 'Fig 6b: Autocorrelation plot of entropy_mean sequence across depths for paraphrastic\n646\t    chains, showing the period-2 oscillation pattern predicted by attractor theory.'\n647\t  analysis: 'This is the experiment that elevates the paper from \"a depth classifier\"\n648\t    to \"an empirical test of distributional dynamics in recursive generation.\" The\n649\t    clean separation between convergent (paraphrastic) and divergent (non-paraphrastic)\n650\t    chains validates the core theoretical insight and connects our work to the ACL\n651\t    2025 attractor cycle literature. H₂ is confirmed for non-paraphrastic chains:\n652\t    drift accumulates monotonically, enabling depth estimation. H₁ is confirmed for\n653\t    paraphrastic chains: convergence limits depth signal to ~2 classes. This dual\n654\t    result explains WHY our approach works and WHY prior work on paraphrasing chains\n655\t    found limited depth discriminability.\n656\t\n657\t    '\n658\t- name: cross_model_chains\n659\t  plan_id: plan_007\n660\t  description: 'Generate cross-model chains where each depth step uses a different\n661\t    generator (randomly sampled from the 5 generators). 1000 seeds × 5 depths. Compares\n662\t    cross-model chain depth classification to single-model chains. More realistic\n663\t    scenario — real web contamination involves diverse generators.\n664\t\n665\t    '\n666\t  expected_results:\n667\t    cross_model_chain_macro_f1: 0.75\n668\t    single_model_chain_macro_f1_mean: 0.85\n669\t    cross_model_gen0_f1: 0.9\n670\t    cross_model_gen1_f1: 0.68\n671\t    cross_model_gen2plus_f1: 0.67\n672\t    cross_model_entropy_drift_per_depth: 0.13\n673\t    cross_vs_single_model_delta: -0.1\n674\t  result_rationale: 'Cross-model chains are harder to classify (F1=0.75 vs single-model\n675\t    0.85) because generator switching introduces distributional discontinuities —\n676\t    each new generator has its own distributional signature that partially masks the\n677\t    monotonic drift signal. However, F1=0.75 is still well above chance (0.33) and\n678\t    practically useful, because the underlying drift phenomenon persists: even when\n679\t    generators change, the cumulative deviation from human text distribution still\n680\t    increases with depth. The gen-1 and gen-2+ classes are most affected (F1=0.68,\n681\t    0.67) because generator-switching noise is most confusing for the middle classes.\n682\t    Gen-0 remains well-separated (F1=0.90) because human text is fundamentally different\n683\t    from any generator''s output. The 10-point drop from single-model is a known cost\n684\t    that should be reported transparently — it bounds the method''s real-world applicability\n685\t    but does not invalidate the approach. Entropy drift rate (0.13 nats/depth) is\n686\t    lower than single-model continuation (0.18) because generator transitions partially\n687\t    reset drift.\n688\t\n689\t    '\n690\t  tables:\n691\t  - name: 'Table 11: Single-Model vs Cross-Model Chain Comparison'\n692\t    columns:\n693\t    - Chain Type\n694\t    - Macro-F1\n695\t    - gen-0 F1\n696\t    - gen-1 F1\n697\t    - gen-2+ F1\n698\t    rows:\n699\t    - - Single-model (mean of 5)\n700\t      - 0.85\n701\t      - 0.95\n702\t      - 0.79\n703\t      - 0.81\n704\t    - - Cross-model (random switch)\n705\t      - 0.75\n706\t      - 0.9\n707\t      - 0.68\n708\t      - 0.67\n709\t    - - Δ\n710\t      - '-0.10'\n711\t      - '-0.05'\n712\t      - '-0.11'\n713\t      - '-0.14'\n714\t  figures_description:\n715\t  - 'Fig 7: Box plot of entropy_mean by depth for cross-model chains. Wider boxes\n716\t    than single-model chains reflect generator-switching variance, but median still\n717\t    increases monotonically.'\n718\t  analysis: 'The 10-point F1 drop is the honest cost of ecological validity. Reviewers\n719\t    (RC4) will ask about this — reporting it transparently strengthens the paper.\n720\t    The key takeaway is that depth signal survives cross-model chains despite generator-switching\n721\t    noise, maintaining F1=0.75. For practical data curation, this means depth-aware\n722\t    filtering still provides value even when contamination sources are diverse. The\n723\t    fact that gen-0 F1 remains at 0.90 means the method''s primary utility (separating\n724\t    human from any-depth synthetic) is robust; the finer-grained depth estimation\n725\t    within synthetic text is where accuracy degrades.\n726\t\n727\t    '\n728\t- name: multi_domain_seeds\n729\t  plan_id: plan_008\n730\t  description: 'Expand seeds to 4 domains: Wikipedia, news (CNN/DailyMail), scientific\n731\t    (S2ORC abstracts), creative writing (WritingPrompts). 500 seeds per domain × 5\n732\t    generators × 5 depths. Tests domain generalization of depth signal.\n733\t\n734\t    '\n735\t  expected_results:\n736\t    wikipedia_macro_f1: 0.86\n737\t    news_macro_f1: 0.83\n738\t    scientific_macro_f1: 0.81\n739\t    creative_macro_f1: 0.78\n740\t    cross_domain_leave_one_out_f1: 0.77\n741\t    pooled_all_domains_f1: 0.84\n742\t    domain_variance_of_f1: 0.0009\n743\t  result_rationale: 'Wikipedia performs best (0.86) because it matches the MVP setting\n744\t    and has the most structured, information-dense text that generators struggle to\n745\t    faithfully continue. News is close (0.83) due to similar factual density. Scientific\n746\t    text (0.81) has domain-specific vocabulary that somewhat masks depth drift in\n747\t    surface features, but reference-model features still capture the signal. Creative\n748\t    writing is hardest (0.78) because generators produce more fluent, human-like creative\n749\t    text — the distributional gap between human and generated creative writing is\n750\t    smaller than for factual domains, reducing the overall drift magnitude. Cross-domain\n751\t    leave-one-out (0.77) confirms that the depth signal generalizes across domains,\n752\t    though some domain-specific calibration is lost. The low variance (0.0009) across\n753\t    domains indicates that depth signal is a robust, domain-general phenomenon. Pooled\n754\t    performance (0.84) suggests that multi-domain training is beneficial — diverse\n755\t    training data helps the classifier learn domain-invariant depth features.\n756\t\n757\t    '\n758\t  tables:\n759\t  - name: 'Table 12: Multi-Domain Depth Classification'\n760\t    columns:\n761\t    - Domain\n762\t    - Seeds\n763\t    - Macro-F1\n764\t    - gen-0 F1\n765\t    - gen-1 F1\n766\t    - gen-2+ F1\n767\t    rows:\n768\t    - - Wikipedia\n769\t      - 500\n770\t      - 0.86\n771\t      - 0.95\n772\t      - 0.8\n773\t      - 0.82\n774\t    - - News\n775\t      - 500\n776\t      - 0.83\n777\t      - 0.93\n778\t      - 0.77\n779\t      - 0.79\n780\t    - - Scientific\n781\t      - 500\n782\t      - 0.81\n783\t      - 0.91\n784\t      - 0.74\n785\t      - 0.77\n786\t    - - Creative\n787\t      - 500\n788\t      - 0.78\n789\t      - 0.88\n790\t      - 0.71\n791\t      - 0.74\n792\t    - - Pooled (all)\n793\t      - 2000\n794\t      - 0.84\n795\t      - 0.93\n796\t      - 0.78\n797\t      - 0.8\n798\t    - - Leave-one-domain-out\n799\t      - —\n800\t      - 0.77\n801\t      - 0.88\n802\t      - 0.7\n803\t      - 0.73\n804\t  figures_description:\n805\t  - 'Fig 8: Faceted violin plot of entropy_mean by depth, one panel per domain. All\n806\t    domains show monotonic drift but with different baseline offsets and drift rates.'\n807\t  analysis: 'Domain generalization confirms that distributional drift is not an artifact\n808\t    of Wikipedia-style text. The ordering (factual > creative) is interpretable and\n809\t    provides nuance for practitioners: depth estimation is most reliable for factual\n810\t    domains where generators have clear distributional signatures. The cross-domain\n811\t    leave-one-out result (0.77) is strong enough to claim domain generalization in\n812\t    the paper. For AAAI, including 4 domains in the evaluation addresses RC6 and demonstrates\n813\t    ecological validity.\n814\t\n815\t    '\n816\t- name: finer_grained_ordinal\n817\t  plan_id: plan_009\n818\t  description: '5-class ordinal classification (gen-0 through gen-4) on the plan_002\n819\t    data. Tests whether non-paraphrastic chains support finer-grained depth estimation\n820\t    beyond the 3-class setup.\n821\t\n822\t    '\n823\t  expected_results:\n824\t    ordinal_5class_macro_f1: 0.62\n825\t    ordinal_5class_gen0_f1: 0.92\n826\t    ordinal_5class_gen1_f1: 0.68\n827\t    ordinal_5class_gen2_f1: 0.52\n828\t    ordinal_5class_gen3_f1: 0.48\n829\t    ordinal_5class_gen4_f1: 0.51\n830\t    ordinal_mae: 0.58\n831\t    ordinal_spearman_rho: 0.83\n832\t    adjacent_class_confusion_pct: 72.0\n833\t  result_rationale: '5-class F1 (0.62) is lower than 3-class (0.86) because deeper\n834\t    classes (gen-2/gen-3/gen-4) have progressively overlapping feature distributions\n835\t    — drift accumulates but with diminishing marginal signal per step. Gen-0 remains\n836\t    easy (F1=0.92), gen-1 is moderate (F1=0.68), and gen-2/3/4 are difficult (F1≈0.48–0.52)\n837\t    because the distributional differences between adjacent deep classes are small.\n838\t    However, the high Spearman ρ (0.83) and low MAE (0.58) indicate that the classifier''s\n839\t    errors are ordinal — when it misclassifies, it predicts an adjacent class (72%\n840\t    of errors are off-by-one), not a distant one. This supports ordinal regression\n841\t    as a natural formulation and suggests that while exact depth prediction is noisy\n842\t    for deep classes, the relative ordering is preserved. For the paper, this result\n843\t    supports the claim that drift is monotonic (H₂) while honestly reporting the practical\n844\t    ceiling on depth resolution.\n845\t\n846\t    '\n847\t  tables:\n848\t  - name: 'Table 13: 5-Class Ordinal Depth Classification'\n849\t    columns:\n850\t    - Class\n851\t    - Precision\n852\t    - Recall\n853\t    - F1\n854\t    rows:\n855\t    - - gen-0\n856\t      - 0.93\n857\t      - 0.91\n858\t      - 0.92\n859\t    - - gen-1\n860\t      - 0.66\n861\t      - 0.7\n862\t      - 0.68\n863\t    - - gen-2\n864\t      - 0.5\n865\t      - 0.54\n866\t      - 0.52\n867\t    - - gen-3\n868\t      - 0.47\n869\t      - 0.49\n870\t      - 0.48\n871\t    - - gen-4\n872\t      - 0.53\n873\t      - 0.5\n874\t      - 0.51\n875\t    - - Macro\n876\t      - —\n877\t      - —\n878\t      - 0.62\n879\t  - name: 'Table 13b: Ordinal Error Analysis'\n880\t    columns:\n881\t    - Metric\n882\t    - Value\n883\t    rows:\n884\t    - - MAE\n885\t      - 0.58\n886\t    - - Spearman ρ (pred vs true depth)\n887\t      - 0.83\n888\t    - - Adjacent-class confusion (%)\n889\t      - 72%\n890\t  figures_description:\n891\t  - 'Fig 9a: 5×5 confusion matrix heatmap. Diagonal + adjacent-diagonal cells are\n892\t    dark, confirming ordinal error structure.'\n893\t  - 'Fig 9b: Predicted depth (box plot) vs true depth. Boxes overlap for gen-2/3/4\n894\t    but medians increase monotonically.'\n895\t  analysis: 'The 5-class result characterizes the resolution ceiling of distributional\n896\t    fingerprints. While exact depth-4 vs depth-3 discrimination is weak, the ordinal\n897\t    structure is strong (ρ=0.83). This has practical implications: for data curation,\n898\t    coarse depth buckets (gen-0 / gen-1 / gen-2+) are more useful than exact depth\n899\t    anyway — the key decision boundary is \"how much drift is tolerable.\" The result\n900\t    also differentiates our work from attractor theory: paraphrastic chains converge\n901\t    to ~2 distinguishable classes, while our non-paraphrastic chains support at least\n902\t    3 clean classes and 5 ordinal classes. This directly addresses RC3.\n903\t\n904\t    '\n905\t- name: human_edit_robustness\n906\t  plan_id: plan_010\n907\t  description: 'Robustness test on plan_002 data with simulated edits: (A) random\n908\t    word replacement at 5%, 10%, 20% rates, (B) LLM-simulated light editing (rephrase\n909\t    random sentences). Tests depth signal persistence under post-generation editing.\n910\t\n911\t    '\n912\t  expected_results:\n913\t    no_edit_macro_f1: 0.86\n914\t    random_5pct_macro_f1: 0.83\n915\t    random_10pct_macro_f1: 0.78\n916\t    random_20pct_macro_f1: 0.67\n917\t    llm_light_edit_macro_f1: 0.76\n918\t    degradation_slope_per_10pct: -0.095\n919\t  result_rationale: 'Random word replacement at 5% has minimal impact (F1=0.83, Δ=-0.03)\n920\t    because replacing 5% of tokens introduces noise uniformly across all depth classes,\n921\t    preserving the relative distributional differences. At 10% (F1=0.78), the noise\n922\t    begins to mask depth-specific features — entropy statistics become noisier as\n923\t    random words inject unpredictable surprise values. At 20% (F1=0.67), substantial\n924\t    text corruption disrupts the distributional fingerprint significantly but performance\n925\t    remains above chance (0.33). LLM-simulated editing (F1=0.76) is comparable to\n926\t    10% random replacement because light rephrasing changes surface statistics but\n927\t    largely preserves the underlying distributional structure — the edited text still\n928\t    occupies the same region of reference-model feature space. The degradation is\n929\t    approximately linear (-0.095 F1 per 10% edit rate), providing a simple model for\n930\t    practitioners to assess signal reliability given estimated editing rates. These\n931\t    results address RC5 while honestly reporting the method''s sensitivity to post-generation\n932\t    modification.\n933\t\n934\t    '\n935\t  tables:\n936\t  - name: 'Table 14: Robustness to Simulated Edits'\n937\t    columns:\n938\t    - Edit Type\n939\t    - Edit Rate\n940\t    - Macro-F1\n941\t    - Δ from No-Edit\n942\t    rows:\n943\t    - - None (baseline)\n944\t      - 0%\n945\t      - 0.86\n946\t      - —\n947\t    - - Random word replacement\n948\t      - 5%\n949\t      - 0.83\n950\t      - '-0.03'\n951\t    - - Random word replacement\n952\t      - 10%\n953\t      - 0.78\n954\t      - '-0.08'\n955\t    - - Random word replacement\n956\t      - 20%\n957\t      - 0.67\n958\t      - '-0.19'\n959\t    - - LLM light editing\n960\t      - ~8–12%\n961\t      - 0.76\n962\t      - '-0.10'\n963\t  figures_description:\n964\t  - 'Fig 10: Line plot — macro-F1 vs edit rate. Linear degradation with 95% CI band.\n965\t    Horizontal dashed line at chance level (0.33). Annotation at 10% edit rate: ''typical\n966\t    human editing range.'''\n967\t  analysis: 'The robustness profile is realistic for practical deployment. Light editing\n968\t    (5–10%) typical of human post-processing preserves most of the depth signal. Heavy\n969\t    editing (20%) degrades performance significantly but remains above chance, suggesting\n970\t    that even heavily edited text retains some distributional fingerprint. For the\n971\t    paper, this experiment provides the necessary caveat about real-world applicability\n972\t    while showing that the method is viable for the most common use case (lightly\n973\t    edited or unedited synthetic text in web-scraped data).\n974\t\n975\t    '\n976\t- name: ld_score_feature\n977\t  plan_id: plan_011\n978\t  description: 'Add LD-Score (character-distribution signature from arXiv:2605.01647)\n979\t    as an additional feature to the plan_001 ablation framework. Tests whether character-level\n980\t    features provide complementary depth signal beyond token-level distributional\n981\t    features.\n982\t\n983\t    '\n984\t  expected_results:\n985\t    full_plus_ld_macro_f1: 0.88\n986\t    ld_score_only_macro_f1: 0.59\n987\t    ld_score_permutation_importance: 0.06\n988\t    full_plus_ld_vs_full_delta: 0.01\n989\t  result_rationale: 'LD-Score alone achieves modest depth discrimination (F1=0.59)\n990\t    because character-level distributional signatures capture some depth-dependent\n991\t    patterns — deeper generation text has slightly more uniform character distributions\n992\t    as vocabulary impoverishment affects character-level statistics. However, adding\n993\t    LD-Score to the full feature set provides only marginal improvement (Δ=+0.01)\n994\t    because LD-Score''s signal is largely redundant with existing surface features\n995\t    (TTR, hapax_ratio) — both measure vocabulary impoverishment, just at different\n996\t    granularities (character vs word level). The low permutation importance (0.06)\n997\t    confirms that LD-Score is not providing new information. This is a useful negative-ish\n998\t    result: it shows that character-level features do not add a fundamentally new\n999\t    signal modality, reinforcing that the key complementary feature families are surface\n1000\t    vocabulary statistics and reference-model distributional features.\n1001\t\n1002\t    '\n1003\t  tables:\n1004\t  - name: 'Table 15: LD-Score Feature Integration'\n1005\t    columns:\n1006\t    - Feature Set\n1007\t    - Macro-F1\n1008\t    - Δ from Full\n1009\t    rows:\n1010\t    - - Full (7 features)\n1011\t      - 0.87\n1012\t      - —\n1013\t    - - Full + LD-Score (8 features)\n1014\t      - 0.88\n1015\t      - '+0.01'\n1016\t    - - LD-Score only\n1017\t      - 0.59\n1018\t      - '-0.28'\n1019\t  figures_description:\n1020\t  - 'Fig S1 (supplementary): Feature importance bar chart with LD-Score included.\n1021\t    LD-Score ranks below all reference-model features.'\n1022\t  analysis: 'A clean but unexciting result that belongs in the supplementary material.\n1023\t    It confirms the two-modality structure of depth signal (reference-model entropy\n1024\t    + surface vocabulary) and shows that adding more surface-level features (even\n1025\t    at a different granularity) does not expand the signal envelope. For the paper,\n1026\t    this addresses potential questions about \"what about other feature types\" and\n1027\t    justifies the focus on reference-model features as the novel contribution.\n1028\t\n1029\t    '\n1030\t- name: end_to_end_classifier\n1031\t  plan_id: plan_012\n1032\t  description: 'QLoRA fine-tune Qwen3.5-0.8B for end-to-end depth classification (directly\n1033\t    from text, no handcrafted features). Compares learned representations vs handcrafted\n1034\t    distributional features on the plan_002 dataset.\n1035\t\n1036\t    '\n1037\t  expected_results:\n1038\t    qwen_qlora_macro_f1: 0.83\n1039\t    qwen_qlora_gen0_f1: 0.94\n1040\t    qwen_qlora_gen1_f1: 0.77\n1041\t    qwen_qlora_gen2plus_f1: 0.79\n1042\t    handcrafted_features_macro_f1: 0.86\n1043\t    qwen_qlora_cross_gen_leave_one_out_f1: 0.71\n1044\t    handcrafted_cross_gen_leave_one_out_f1: 0.78\n1045\t    qwen_qlora_training_gpu_hours: 12.0\n1046\t    handcrafted_training_time_minutes: 3.0\n1047\t  result_rationale: 'The QLoRA fine-tuned model achieves competitive in-distribution\n1048\t    performance (F1=0.83 vs handcrafted 0.86) because the transformer can implicitly\n1049\t    learn distributional features from raw text. However, it underperforms the handcrafted\n1050\t    approach for two reasons: (1) the handcrafted features distill the reference model''s\n1051\t    perspective into compact statistics that directly measure what matters (distributional\n1052\t    drift from human text), while the fine-tuned model must learn this implicitly\n1053\t    from text patterns; (2) the XGBoost classifier on 7 features is harder to overfit\n1054\t    than a 0.8B parameter model on ~25K training examples. The larger gap in cross-generator\n1055\t    leave-one-out (0.71 vs 0.78) reveals that the fine-tuned model memorizes generator-specific\n1056\t    artifacts more than the handcrafted features, which are designed to be generator-invariant.\n1057\t    The 4000× training time difference (12 GPU-hours vs 3 minutes) makes the handcrafted\n1058\t    approach clearly preferable for practical deployment. This result validates the\n1059\t    feature engineering approach over brute-force fine-tuning and justifies the paper''s\n1060\t    focus on interpretable distributional features.\n1061\t\n1062\t    '\n1063\t  tables:\n1064\t  - name: 'Table 16: End-to-End vs Feature-Based Comparison'\n1065\t    columns:\n1066\t    - Method\n1067\t    - In-Dist F1\n1068\t    - Cross-Gen F1\n1069\t    - Training Cost\n1070\t    rows:\n1071\t    - - XGBoost + handcrafted features\n1072\t      - 0.86\n1073\t      - 0.78\n1074\t      - 3 min\n1075\t    - - QLoRA Qwen3.5-0.8B\n1076\t      - 0.83\n1077\t      - 0.71\n1078\t      - 12 GPU·h\n1079\t    - - Δ (handcrafted − QLoRA)\n1080\t      - '+0.03'\n1081\t      - '+0.07'\n1082\t      - —\n1083\t  figures_description:\n1084\t  - 'Fig S2 (supplementary): Paired bar chart — in-distribution vs cross-generator\n1085\t    F1 for both methods. Handcrafted features show smaller generalization gap.'\n1086\t  analysis: 'This is a strategically important result. Reviewers might ask \"why not\n1087\t    just fine-tune a model?\" — this experiment shows that handcrafted features outperform,\n1088\t    generalize better, are 4000× cheaper to train, and are interpretable (you can\n1089\t    point to specific features and explain why they work). The fine-tuned model''s\n1090\t    worse cross-generator generalization is the most telling result: it confirms that\n1091\t    the handcrafted features capture the right abstraction (distributional drift as\n1092\t    measured by a reference model) while the fine-tuned model takes shortcuts via\n1093\t    generator-specific patterns. This result belongs in the supplementary material\n1094\t    and reinforces the paper''s methodological choice.'\n1095\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"176da81f-308c-4dd6-ae97-a751123aff6c","uuid":"3547d64f-260c-4d0d-8915-2a11b93792d3","timestamp":"2026-05-14T18:40:58.292Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_3/projects/gen-depth-fingerprint/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T181213/best/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Original framing fully intact. Story: non-paraphrastic chains\n  exhibit monotonic distributional divergence (H₂ confirmed), detectable via reference-model\n  entropy features that capture drift beyond surface vocabulary impoverishment. Headline:\n  \"distributional fingerprints enable risk-stratified data curation,\" with clean ablation\n  separating signal from shortcut. All three claims stated at full strength. AAAI\n  submission with no scope reduction.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: feature_ablation\n  plan_id: plan_001\n  description: 'Ablation on existing MVP data (4609 records, Pythia-2.8B generator,\n    Pythia-410M reference, Wikipedia seeds). Three conditions: (A) reference-only\n    features (entropy_mean, entropy_std, entropy_min, entropy_max, tail_decay), (B)\n    surface-only features (ttr, hapax_ratio), (C) full 7-feature set. Same XGBoost\n    classifier, 5-fold stratified CV, 3-class (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    ref_only_macro_f1: 0.79\n    ref_only_cv_std: 0.018\n    ref_only_gen0_f1: 0.91\n    ref_only_gen1_f1: 0.71\n    ref_only_gen2plus_f1: 0.74\n    surface_only_macro_f1: 0.63\n    surface_only_cv_std: 0.022\n    surface_only_gen0_f1: 0.82\n    surface_only_gen1_f1: 0.52\n    surface_only_gen2plus_f1: 0.55\n    full_macro_f1: 0.87\n    full_cv_std: 0.012\n    full_gen0_f1: 0.96\n    full_gen1_f1: 0.81\n    full_gen2plus_f1: 0.83\n    ref_minus_surface_delta: 0.16\n    mcnemar_p_ref_vs_surface: 3.0e-05\n    permutation_importance_entropy_mean: 0.28\n    permutation_importance_entropy_std: 0.19\n    permutation_importance_hapax_ratio: 0.14\n    permutation_importance_ttr: 0.09\n  result_rationale: 'MVP feature importance (hapax_ratio=0.30) was inflated by XGBoost''s\n    split-gain metric, which overweights high-cardinality continuous features with\n    many useful split points. When surface features are ablated, the 5 reference-model\n    features retain macro-F1=0.79 because they directly measure the generator''s deviation\n    from the reference model''s next-token distribution — a signal that accumulates\n    monotonically with depth as the generator''s output drifts further from human\n    text. Entropy_mean captures the average surprise under the reference model (increases\n    with depth as generated text occupies lower-probability regions), while entropy_std\n    captures the increasing variance of surprise (deeper text alternates between familiar\n    patterns and novel drift). Surface features alone (F1=0.63) capture only vocabulary\n    impoverishment — TTR and hapax_ratio decline with depth as generators recycle\n    high-frequency tokens — but this is a weaker, noisier proxy. The 0.16 delta and\n    significant McNemar test confirm reference features carry independent, dominant\n    signal. The full set achieves F1=0.87 (up from MVP''s 0.84) because retuned hyperparameters\n    on the same data with proper ablation awareness extract slightly more complementary\n    value. Permutation importance (which avoids split-gain bias) reranks entropy_mean\n    as the top feature.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 1: Feature Ablation Results (3-class depth classification)'\n    columns:\n    - Feature Set\n    - Features\n    - Macro-F1\n    - gen-0 F1\n    - gen-1 F1\n    - gen-2+ F1\n    - CV std\n    rows:\n    - - Reference-only\n      - entropy_{mean,std,min,max}, tail_decay\n      - 0.79\n      - 0.91\n      - 0.71\n      - 0.74\n      - 0.018\n    - - Surface-only\n      - ttr, hapax_ratio\n      - 0.63\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.52\n      - 0.55\n      - 0.022\n    - - Full\n      - All 7 features\n      - 0.87\n      - 0.96\n      - 0.81\n      - 0.83\n      - 0.012\n    - - Δ (ref − surface)\n      - —\n      - '+0.16'\n      - '+0.09'\n      - '+0.19'\n      - '+0.19'\n      - —\n  - name: 'Table 2: Permutation Importance (Full model, 5-fold mean)'\n    columns:\n    - Feature\n    - Permutation Importance\n    - XGBoost Gain (MVP)\n    rows:\n    - - entropy_mean\n      - 0.28\n      - 0.22\n    - - entropy_std\n      - 0.19\n      - 0.13\n    - - hapax_ratio\n      - 0.14\n      - 0.3\n    - - tail_decay\n      - 0.13\n      - 0.06\n    - - ttr\n      - 0.09\n      - 0.17\n    - - entropy_max\n      - 0.09\n      - 0.07\n    - - entropy_min\n      - 0.08\n      - 0.06\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 1a: Grouped bar chart — Macro-F1 for ref-only / surface-only / full, with\n    95% CI error bars from CV. Clear visual gap between ref-only and surface-only.'\n  - 'Fig 1b: Per-class F1 heatmap (3 feature sets × 3 classes). Shows that the surface→ref\n    gap is largest for gen-1 and gen-2+ (the harder classes), confirming reference\n    features add discriminability precisely where surface shortcuts fail.'\n  - 'Fig 1c: Permutation importance bar chart (full model) vs XGBoost gain bar chart\n    (MVP). Side-by-side comparison reveals hapax_ratio''s gain was inflated; entropy_mean\n    is the true top feature under permutation.'\n  analysis: 'The ablation cleanly validates Claim 2. The ref-only advantage is strongest\n    on gen-1 (Δ=+0.19) and gen-2+ (Δ=+0.19), the classes where surface features struggle\n    most — TTR/hapax can separate human text from any synthetic text but cannot distinguish\n    depth levels within synthetic text, because vocabulary impoverishment saturates\n    quickly (mostly between gen-0→gen-1). Reference-model entropy features, by contrast,\n    capture cumulative distributional drift that continues to grow between gen-1 and\n    gen-2+. The reranking of feature importance under permutation (entropy_mean rises\n    to 0.28, hapax_ratio drops to 0.14) directly addresses RC1 (\"Is this just vocabulary\n    impoverishment?\") — it is not. The full model''s improvement to 0.87 suggests\n    the two feature families capture partially orthogonal information: surface features\n    provide a cheap gen-0 vs rest shortcut, while reference features provide the finer\n    gen-1 vs gen-2+ signal.\n\n    '\n- name: cross_generator_evaluation\n  plan_id: plan_002\n  description: 'Generate 25K chains: 1000 Wikipedia seeds × 5 generators (Pythia-2.8B,\n    OLMo-2-1B, Qwen3.5-2B, Qwen3.5-0.8B, Pythia-1B) × 5 depths. Feature extraction\n    with Pythia-410M reference model. Leave-one-generator-out CV (train on 4 generators,\n    test on held-out generator). Also report per-generator in-distribution results\n    and per-class breakdowns.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    leave_one_out_macro_f1_mean: 0.78\n    leave_one_out_macro_f1_std: 0.03\n    leave_one_out_gen1_f1_mean: 0.73\n    leave_one_out_gen2plus_f1_mean: 0.75\n    leave_one_out_gen0_f1_mean: 0.88\n    in_distribution_5fold_macro_f1: 0.86\n    per_generator_held_out:\n      pythia_2_8b: 0.81\n      olmo_2_1b: 0.77\n      qwen3_5_2b: 0.76\n      qwen3_5_0_8b: 0.74\n      pythia_1b: 0.82\n    total_chains_generated: 24200\n    attrition_rate_pct: 3.2\n    generation_gpu_hours: 18.5\n  result_rationale: 'Cross-generator generalization holds (mean leave-one-out F1=0.78)\n    because distributional drift is measured from the reference model''s perspective,\n    not the generator''s. All generators produce text that progressively deviates\n    from human text distribution as measured by Pythia-410M; the drift trajectory\n    differs in magnitude but shares the same directional pattern (entropy increases,\n    tail probability mass shifts). Pythia-family generators (2.8B, 1B) transfer best\n    to each other (F1=0.81–0.82) due to shared pretraining distribution. Qwen3.5-0.8B\n    is the hardest held-out case (F1=0.74) because its smaller capacity produces qualitatively\n    different text that drifts faster at early depths — the classifier trained on\n    larger models underestimates Qwen-0.8B''s depth. The gen-1 vs gen-2+ boundary\n    remains the bottleneck (gen-1 F1=0.73) because gen-1 text from some generators\n    overlaps with gen-2+ text from others in feature space. In-distribution pooled\n    performance (0.86) is close to MVP (0.84) despite mixing generators, confirming\n    that generator diversity adds training signal rather than noise. Attrition (~3.2%)\n    is lower than MVP (3.7%) due to improved generation pipeline with retry logic.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 3: Leave-One-Generator-Out Cross-Validation'\n    columns:\n    - Held-out Generator\n    - Macro-F1\n    - gen-0 F1\n    - gen-1 F1\n    - gen-2+ F1\n    rows:\n    - - Pythia-2.8B\n      - 0.81\n      - 0.91\n      - 0.75\n      - 0.77\n    - - OLMo-2-1B\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.87\n      - 0.72\n      - 0.73\n    - - Qwen3.5-2B\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.86\n      - 0.71\n      - 0.72\n    - - Qwen3.5-0.8B\n      - 0.74\n      - 0.84\n      - 0.68\n      - 0.7\n    - - Pythia-1B\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.92\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.78\n    - - Mean ± std\n      - 0.78 ± 0.03\n      - 0.88 ± 0.03\n      - 0.73 ± 0.03\n      - 0.75 ± 0.03\n  - name: 'Table 4: Per-Generator In-Distribution 5-Fold CV'\n    columns:\n    - Generator\n    - Macro-F1\n    - CV std\n    rows:\n    - - Pythia-2.8B\n      - 0.85\n      - 0.014\n    - - OLMo-2-1B\n      - 0.84\n      - 0.016\n    - - Qwen3.5-2B\n      - 0.86\n      - 0.013\n    - - Qwen3.5-0.8B\n      - 0.83\n      - 0.019\n    - - Pythia-1B\n      - 0.84\n      - 0.015\n    - - Pooled (all 5)\n      - 0.86\n      - 0.011\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 2a: Leave-one-out macro-F1 bar chart with error bars, generators ordered\n    by held-out performance. Horizontal dashed line at 0.78 (mean). Shows Pythia-family\n    clusters above mean, Qwen-0.8B below.'\n  - 'Fig 2b: Confusion matrix heatmaps (5 panels, one per held-out generator). Highlights\n    that gen-1↔gen-2+ confusion is the primary failure mode across all generators.'\n  - 'Fig 2c: t-SNE of reference-model features colored by depth, faceted by generator.\n    Shows that depth clusters are geometrically similar across generators despite\n    generator-specific offsets in feature space.'\n  analysis: 'The 0.78 mean leave-one-out F1 with narrow std (0.03) strongly supports\n    Claim 1''s cross-generator generalization. The pattern is consistent: gen-0 is\n    always well-separated (F1≥0.84), while gen-1 and gen-2+ form a harder boundary.\n    Pythia-family transfer is strongest, suggesting some same-family distributional\n    similarity, but even cross-family transfer (Pythia→Qwen) maintains F1≥0.74. This\n    confirms the distributional fingerprint is a property of the generation process,\n    not a generator-specific artifact. The pooled in-distribution result (0.86) exceeding\n    any single-generator result suggests that multi-generator training improves robustness.\n    For the paper, this table is the centerpiece of Claim 1 and directly addresses\n    RC2.\n\n    '\n- name: cross_family_reference_model\n  plan_id: plan_003\n  description: 'Re-extract features using non-Pythia reference models (OLMo-2-1B,\n    Qwen3.5-0.8B) on the plan_002 dataset (25K chains). Compare classification performance\n    across 3 reference model families. Tests whether the fingerprint is reference-model-agnostic.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    pythia_410m_ref_macro_f1: 0.86\n    olmo_2_1b_ref_macro_f1: 0.76\n    qwen3_5_0_8b_ref_macro_f1: 0.74\n    ensemble_3refs_macro_f1: 0.88\n    cross_ref_leave_one_out_macro_f1: 0.75\n    feature_correlation_pythia_olmo_entropy_mean: 0.82\n    feature_correlation_pythia_qwen_entropy_mean: 0.78\n  result_rationale: 'All three reference models detect depth signal (F1≥0.74) because\n    any well-trained LM assigns progressively lower probability to deeper-generation\n    text — the drift is a property of the text, not the measuring instrument. Pythia-410M\n    performs best (0.86) because it was the reference model used during pipeline development\n    and its capacity is calibrated for sensitivity to distributional shifts (small\n    enough to be \"surprised\" by drift, large enough to model human text well). OLMo-2-1B\n    (0.76) and Qwen3.5-0.8B (0.74) are slightly weaker because their different pretraining\n    distributions create different baselines for \"expected\" text, adding noise to\n    depth features. High cross-reference entropy_mean correlations (0.78–0.82) confirm\n    that all three models agree on the direction of drift even if magnitudes differ.\n    The 3-reference ensemble (0.88) exceeds any single reference, indicating that\n    diverse reference models capture complementary aspects of distributional drift.\n    This decisively addresses RC2 — the signal is not a Pythia-specific artifact.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 5: Reference Model Comparison (pooled 5-generator data)'\n    columns:\n    - Reference Model\n    - Family\n    - Params\n    - Macro-F1\n    - gen-0 F1\n    - gen-1 F1\n    - gen-2+ F1\n    rows:\n    - - Pythia-410M\n      - Pythia\n      - 410M\n      - 0.86\n      - 0.95\n      - 0.8\n      - 0.82\n    - - OLMo-2-1B\n      - OLMo\n      - 1B\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.88\n      - 0.69\n      - 0.72\n    - - Qwen3.5-0.8B\n      - Qwen\n      - 0.8B\n      - 0.74\n      - 0.86\n      - 0.66\n      - 0.7\n    - - Ensemble (3 refs)\n      - Mixed\n      - —\n      - 0.88\n      - 0.96\n      - 0.83\n      - 0.85\n  - name: 'Table 6: Pairwise Feature Correlation (Spearman ρ) Across Reference Models'\n    columns:\n    - Feature\n    - Pythia↔OLMo\n    - Pythia↔Qwen\n    - OLMo↔Qwen\n    rows:\n    - - entropy_mean\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.8\n    - - entropy_std\n      - 0.79\n      - 0.74\n      - 0.76\n    - - tail_decay\n      - 0.71\n      - 0.66\n      - 0.69\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 3a: Parallel coordinates plot showing entropy_mean distributions by depth,\n    one panel per reference model. Monotonic increase with depth visible in all three,\n    confirming H₂ across reference architectures.'\n  - 'Fig 3b: Scatter matrix of entropy_mean values across reference model pairs, colored\n    by depth. Strong positive correlation with depth-dependent clustering confirms\n    cross-reference consistency.'\n  analysis: 'The cross-family validation eliminates the concern that Pythia-410M has\n    some idiosyncratic sensitivity to synthetic text. All three reference architectures\n    detect monotonic distributional drift. The ensemble''s improvement to 0.88 provides\n    an optional \"best-effort\" configuration for practitioners, though the single-reference\n    Pythia-410M result (0.86) is sufficient for the paper''s claims. The correlation\n    analysis (Table 6) is particularly informative: high but imperfect correlations\n    indicate that reference models measure the same underlying phenomenon through\n    slightly different lenses, explaining why ensembling helps. For the paper, this\n    experiment transforms a potential weakness (Pythia-only evaluation) into a strength\n    (reference-model-agnostic signal).\n\n    '\n- name: downstream_utility\n  plan_id: plan_004\n  description: 'Fine-tune Pythia-410M on depth-stratified subsets of the plan_002\n    data. Measure perplexity on held-out human test set as function of training data\n    depth composition. Compare depth-aware filtering (keep gen-0 + gen-1, discard\n    gen-2+) vs binary baselines (FastDetectGPT threshold, LD-Score threshold) for\n    preserving downstream model quality.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    depth_quality_spearman_rho: 0.72\n    ppl_gen0_only: 28.3\n    ppl_gen1_only: 30.1\n    ppl_gen2_only: 34.7\n    ppl_gen3_only: 38.9\n    ppl_gen4_only: 43.2\n    ppl_mixed_uniform: 35.8\n    ppl_depth_filtered_gen01: 29.0\n    ppl_binary_fastdetectgpt: 31.8\n    ppl_binary_ldscore: 32.1\n    ppl_human_baseline: 27.5\n    depth_filter_vs_binary_gap_pct: 5.2\n    training_samples_kept_depth_filter: 0.42\n    training_samples_kept_binary: 0.28\n  result_rationale: 'Perplexity increases monotonically with training data depth (28.3→43.2\n    from gen-0→gen-4) because deeper-generation text has compounded distributional\n    drift from human text — fine-tuning on it shifts the model''s learned distribution\n    away from the human test set. The Spearman ρ=0.72 between depth and perplexity\n    is strong but not perfect because individual samples vary in drift magnitude (some\n    gen-3 texts are less drifted than some gen-2 texts). Depth-aware filtering (keep\n    gen-0+gen-1, perplexity=29.0) outperforms binary baselines (~31.8–32.1) by 5.2%\n    because binary detectors discard all synthetic text including useful gen-1 content\n    that is distributionally close to human text. Binary filtering retains only 28%\n    of data (mostly gen-0) while depth filtering retains 42% (gen-0 + gen-1), providing\n    more training data of acceptable quality. The gap between gen-0-only (28.3) and\n    depth-filtered (29.0) is small, confirming that gen-1 text is nearly as good as\n    human text for training — the key practical insight for data curation.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 7: Fine-tuning Perplexity by Training Data Depth'\n    columns:\n    - Training Data\n    - Depth Composition\n    - PPL (↓)\n    - Samples Used\n    rows:\n    - - Human-only\n      - gen-0\n      - 28.3\n      - ~4800\n    - - gen-1 only\n      - gen-1\n      - 30.1\n      - ~4750\n    - - gen-2 only\n      - gen-2\n      - 34.7\n      - ~4500\n    - - gen-3 only\n      - gen-3\n      - 38.9\n      - ~4200\n    - - gen-4 only\n      - gen-4\n      - 43.2\n      - ~3900\n    - - Uniform mix\n      - gen-0–4 equal\n      - 35.8\n      - ~22000\n    - - Depth-filtered (ours)\n      - gen-0 + gen-1\n      - 29.0\n      - ~9500\n    - - FastDetectGPT filter\n      - binary threshold\n      - 31.8\n      - ~6300\n    - - LD-Score filter\n      - binary threshold\n      - 32.1\n      - ~6100\n    - - Human test baseline\n      - —\n      - 27.5\n      - —\n  - name: 'Table 8: Depth-Aware vs Binary Filtering Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - PPL\n    - Data Retained (%)\n    - PPL Gap to Human-Only\n    rows:\n    - - Depth-filtered (gen-0+1)\n      - 29.0\n      - 42%\n      - '+0.7'\n    - - FastDetectGPT binary\n      - 31.8\n      - 28%\n      - '+3.5'\n    - - LD-Score binary\n      - 32.1\n      - 26%\n      - '+3.8'\n    - - No filtering (all data)\n      - 35.8\n      - 100%\n      - '+7.5'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 4a: Line plot — perplexity vs generation depth (gen-0 through gen-4). Monotonic\n    increase with 95% CI bands from 3 random seeds. Confirms H₂''s downstream consequence.'\n  - 'Fig 4b: Bar chart comparing filtering strategies (depth-aware, FastDetectGPT,\n    LD-Score, no filter) on perplexity and data retention. Dual y-axis: bars for perplexity,\n    overlaid line for % data retained.'\n  - 'Fig 4c: Scatter plot — predicted depth score vs actual fine-tuning perplexity\n    contribution per sample, with Spearman ρ annotation. Shows the sample-level correlation\n    between depth estimate and quality impact.'\n  analysis: 'This experiment validates Claim 3 and provides the practical punchline\n    of the paper. The monotonic perplexity curve (Fig 4a) is the most intuitive visualization\n    of H₂ — it translates distributional drift into a metric practitioners care about.\n    The 5.2% gap between depth-aware and binary filtering is meaningful because it\n    comes with MORE data retained (42% vs 28%), resolving the precision-recall tradeoff\n    that plagues binary detection. The key insight is that gen-1 text is \"good enough\"\n    for training (PPL=30.1 vs human 28.3), but gen-2+ is not (PPL≥34.7). Binary detectors\n    cannot make this distinction. For AAAI reviewers, this experiment connects the\n    theoretical contribution (distributional drift characterization) to a practical\n    application (data curation), which is important for venue fit.\n\n    '\n- name: prompt_type_comparison\n  plan_id: plan_005\n  description: 'Generate chains with 3 prompt types (continuation, elaboration, inspired_rewriting)\n    using Pythia-2.8B on 1000 Wikipedia seeds × 5 depths. Compare depth signal preservation\n    across prompt types. Tests whether semantic divergence from source correlates\n    with depth signal.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    continuation_macro_f1: 0.85\n    elaboration_macro_f1: 0.8\n    inspired_rewriting_macro_f1: 0.72\n    continuation_entropy_drift_per_depth: 0.18\n    elaboration_entropy_drift_per_depth: 0.14\n    inspired_rewriting_entropy_drift_per_depth: 0.1\n    cross_prompt_train_test_macro_f1: 0.74\n    prompt_type_drift_rate_spearman: 0.91\n  result_rationale: 'Continuation preserves the strongest depth signal (F1=0.85) because\n    each generation step continues directly from the previous output, maximizing the\n    accumulation of distributional drift. Elaboration (F1=0.80) injects some new semantic\n    direction via the prompt instruction, which partially resets the drift trajectory\n    at each step — drift still accumulates but at a slower rate. Inspired rewriting\n    (F1=0.72) has the most semantic freedom: the model is instructed to produce text\n    \"inspired by\" the input, allowing it to diverge from the drift trajectory and\n    partially re-anchor to its pretraining distribution. The strong correlation between\n    per-depth entropy drift rate and classification F1 (Spearman ρ=0.91) confirms\n    the theoretical prediction: more constrained generation → faster drift accumulation\n    → stronger depth signal. Cross-prompt-type transfer (F1=0.74) is moderate, indicating\n    that the drift signature is partially shared across prompt types but the rate\n    differences create distribution shift in feature space.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 9: Depth Classification by Prompt Type'\n    columns:\n    - Prompt Type\n    - Macro-F1\n    - gen-0 F1\n    - gen-1 F1\n    - gen-2+ F1\n    - Entropy Drift Rate\n    rows:\n    - - Continuation\n      - 0.85\n      - 0.95\n      - 0.79\n      - 0.81\n      - 0.18 nats/depth\n    - - Elaboration\n      - 0.8\n      - 0.92\n      - 0.73\n      - 0.75\n      - 0.14 nats/depth\n    - - Inspired rewriting\n      - 0.72\n      - 0.86\n      - 0.64\n      - 0.67\n      - 0.10 nats/depth\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 5a: Line plots — entropy_mean vs depth for each prompt type, showing different\n    drift rates. Continuation has steepest slope.'\n  - 'Fig 5b: Scatter plot — prompt type drift rate vs macro-F1, confirming linear\n    relationship. Three points with regression line and ρ annotation.'\n  analysis: 'This experiment decomposes the depth signal by generation modality. The\n    ordering (continuation > elaboration > inspired rewriting) aligns with the theoretical\n    framework: the more the generation task constrains the output to build on previous\n    text, the more drift accumulates. Inspired rewriting''s lower F1 is not a failure\n    — it confirms the mechanism. For the paper, this comparison strengthens the theoretical\n    story by showing that depth signal is graded, not binary, and correlates with\n    the degree of semantic coupling between successive generation steps.\n\n    '\n- name: paraphrastic_comparison\n  plan_id: plan_006\n  description: 'Construct parallel paraphrasing chains (same 1000 seeds, Pythia-2.8B,\n    5 depths) alongside continuation chains. Measure whether paraphrastic chains converge\n    (per attractor theory) while continuation chains diverge (our H₂). Direct empirical\n    test of H₁ vs H₂.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    paraphrase_macro_f1_3class: 0.58\n    paraphrase_gen0_f1: 0.81\n    paraphrase_gen1_f1: 0.46\n    paraphrase_gen2plus_f1: 0.47\n    continuation_macro_f1_3class: 0.85\n    paraphrase_entropy_drift_depth3: 0.06\n    continuation_entropy_drift_depth3: 0.52\n    paraphrase_cycle_detection_pct: 68.0\n    divergence_test_ks_statistic: 0.41\n    divergence_test_p_value: 1.0e-05\n  result_rationale: 'Paraphrastic chains show weak depth signal (F1=0.58) because\n    the semantic preservation constraint of paraphrasing creates attractor dynamics\n    — the text oscillates around a fixed semantic point, and distributional features\n    converge after ~2 steps (confirming ACL 2025 attractor cycle theory). By depth\n    3, paraphrastic entropy drift has nearly plateaued (0.06 nats) while continuation\n    drift continues growing (0.52 nats). The 68% cycle detection rate in paraphrastic\n    chains (text at depth d is more similar to depth d-2 than d-1) directly corroborates\n    the 2-period attractor cycle prediction. Continuation chains show no such periodicity\n    — drift is monotonic. The KS test between depth-3 entropy distributions of paraphrastic\n    vs continuation chains (D=0.41, p<0.001) provides the statistical test of H₁ vs\n    H₂. This is the most theoretically clean result in the paper: H₂ (divergence)\n    holds for non-paraphrastic chains, H₁ (convergence) holds for paraphrastic chains,\n    exactly as predicted by the semantic constraint difference.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 10: Paraphrastic vs Continuation Chain Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - Chain Type\n    - Macro-F1\n    - Entropy Drift @depth3\n    - Cycle Detection %\n    rows:\n    - - Continuation\n      - 0.85\n      - 0.52 nats\n      - < 5%\n    - - Paraphrasing\n      - 0.58\n      - 0.06 nats\n      - 68%\n    - - Δ\n      - '+0.27'\n      - +0.46 nats\n      - —\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 6a: Dual-panel line plot — entropy_mean vs depth for continuation (diverging)\n    and paraphrasing (converging). The visual contrast is the paper''s most compelling\n    figure.'\n  - 'Fig 6b: Autocorrelation plot of entropy_mean sequence across depths for paraphrastic\n    chains, showing the period-2 oscillation pattern predicted by attractor theory.'\n  analysis: 'This is the experiment that elevates the paper from \"a depth classifier\"\n    to \"an empirical test of distributional dynamics in recursive generation.\" The\n    clean separation between convergent (paraphrastic) and divergent (non-paraphrastic)\n    chains validates the core theoretical insight and connects our work to the ACL\n    2025 attractor cycle literature. H₂ is confirmed for non-paraphrastic chains:\n    drift accumulates monotonically, enabling depth estimation. H₁ is confirmed for\n    paraphrastic chains: convergence limits depth signal to ~2 classes. This dual\n    result explains WHY our approach works and WHY prior work on paraphrasing chains\n    found limited depth discriminability.\n\n    '\n- name: cross_model_chains\n  plan_id: plan_007\n  description: 'Generate cross-model chains where each depth step uses a different\n    generator (randomly sampled from the 5 generators). 1000 seeds × 5 depths. Compares\n    cross-model chain depth classification to single-model chains. More realistic\n    scenario — real web contamination involves diverse generators.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    cross_model_chain_macro_f1: 0.75\n    single_model_chain_macro_f1_mean: 0.85\n    cross_model_gen0_f1: 0.9\n    cross_model_gen1_f1: 0.68\n    cross_model_gen2plus_f1: 0.67\n    cross_model_entropy_drift_per_depth: 0.13\n    cross_vs_single_model_delta: -0.1\n  result_rationale: 'Cross-model chains are harder to classify (F1=0.75 vs single-model\n    0.85) because generator switching introduces distributional discontinuities —\n    each new generator has its own distributional signature that partially masks the\n    monotonic drift signal. However, F1=0.75 is still well above chance (0.33) and\n    practically useful, because the underlying drift phenomenon persists: even when\n    generators change, the cumulative deviation from human text distribution still\n    increases with depth. The gen-1 and gen-2+ classes are most affected (F1=0.68,\n    0.67) because generator-switching noise is most confusing for the middle classes.\n    Gen-0 remains well-separated (F1=0.90) because human text is fundamentally different\n    from any generator''s output. The 10-point drop from single-model is a known cost\n    that should be reported transparently — it bounds the method''s real-world applicability\n    but does not invalidate the approach. Entropy drift rate (0.13 nats/depth) is\n    lower than single-model continuation (0.18) because generator transitions partially\n    reset drift.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 11: Single-Model vs Cross-Model Chain Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - Chain Type\n    - Macro-F1\n    - gen-0 F1\n    - gen-1 F1\n    - gen-2+ F1\n    rows:\n    - - Single-model (mean of 5)\n      - 0.85\n      - 0.95\n      - 0.79\n      - 0.81\n    - - Cross-model (random switch)\n      - 0.75\n      - 0.9\n      - 0.68\n      - 0.67\n    - - Δ\n      - '-0.10'\n      - '-0.05'\n      - '-0.11'\n      - '-0.14'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 7: Box plot of entropy_mean by depth for cross-model chains. Wider boxes\n    than single-model chains reflect generator-switching variance, but median still\n    increases monotonically.'\n  analysis: 'The 10-point F1 drop is the honest cost of ecological validity. Reviewers\n    (RC4) will ask about this — reporting it transparently strengthens the paper.\n    The key takeaway is that depth signal survives cross-model chains despite generator-switching\n    noise, maintaining F1=0.75. For practical data curation, this means depth-aware\n    filtering still provides value even when contamination sources are diverse. The\n    fact that gen-0 F1 remains at 0.90 means the method''s primary utility (separating\n    human from any-depth synthetic) is robust; the finer-grained depth estimation\n    within synthetic text is where accuracy degrades.\n\n    '\n- name: multi_domain_seeds\n  plan_id: plan_008\n  description: 'Expand seeds to 4 domains: Wikipedia, news (CNN/DailyMail), scientific\n    (S2ORC abstracts), creative writing (WritingPrompts). 500 seeds per domain × 5\n    generators × 5 depths. Tests domain generalization of depth signal.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    wikipedia_macro_f1: 0.86\n    news_macro_f1: 0.83\n    scientific_macro_f1: 0.81\n    creative_macro_f1: 0.78\n    cross_domain_leave_one_out_f1: 0.77\n    pooled_all_domains_f1: 0.84\n    domain_variance_of_f1: 0.0009\n  result_rationale: 'Wikipedia performs best (0.86) because it matches the MVP setting\n    and has the most structured, information-dense text that generators struggle to\n    faithfully continue. News is close (0.83) due to similar factual density. Scientific\n    text (0.81) has domain-specific vocabulary that somewhat masks depth drift in\n    surface features, but reference-model features still capture the signal. Creative\n    writing is hardest (0.78) because generators produce more fluent, human-like creative\n    text — the distributional gap between human and generated creative writing is\n    smaller than for factual domains, reducing the overall drift magnitude. Cross-domain\n    leave-one-out (0.77) confirms that the depth signal generalizes across domains,\n    though some domain-specific calibration is lost. The low variance (0.0009) across\n    domains indicates that depth signal is a robust, domain-general phenomenon. Pooled\n    performance (0.84) suggests that multi-domain training is beneficial — diverse\n    training data helps the classifier learn domain-invariant depth features.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 12: Multi-Domain Depth Classification'\n    columns:\n    - Domain\n    - Seeds\n    - Macro-F1\n    - gen-0 F1\n    - gen-1 F1\n    - gen-2+ F1\n    rows:\n    - - Wikipedia\n      - 500\n      - 0.86\n      - 0.95\n      - 0.8\n      - 0.82\n    - - News\n      - 500\n      - 0.83\n      - 0.93\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.79\n    - - Scientific\n      - 500\n      - 0.81\n      - 0.91\n      - 0.74\n      - 0.77\n    - - Creative\n      - 500\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.88\n      - 0.71\n      - 0.74\n    - - Pooled (all)\n      - 2000\n      - 0.84\n      - 0.93\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.8\n    - - Leave-one-domain-out\n      - —\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.88\n      - 0.7\n      - 0.73\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 8: Faceted violin plot of entropy_mean by depth, one panel per domain. All\n    domains show monotonic drift but with different baseline offsets and drift rates.'\n  analysis: 'Domain generalization confirms that distributional drift is not an artifact\n    of Wikipedia-style text. The ordering (factual > creative) is interpretable and\n    provides nuance for practitioners: depth estimation is most reliable for factual\n    domains where generators have clear distributional signatures. The cross-domain\n    leave-one-out result (0.77) is strong enough to claim domain generalization in\n    the paper. For AAAI, including 4 domains in the evaluation addresses RC6 and demonstrates\n    ecological validity.\n\n    '\n- name: finer_grained_ordinal\n  plan_id: plan_009\n  description: '5-class ordinal classification (gen-0 through gen-4) on the plan_002\n    data. Tests whether non-paraphrastic chains support finer-grained depth estimation\n    beyond the 3-class setup.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    ordinal_5class_macro_f1: 0.62\n    ordinal_5class_gen0_f1: 0.92\n    ordinal_5class_gen1_f1: 0.68\n    ordinal_5class_gen2_f1: 0.52\n    ordinal_5class_gen3_f1: 0.48\n    ordinal_5class_gen4_f1: 0.51\n    ordinal_mae: 0.58\n    ordinal_spearman_rho: 0.83\n    adjacent_class_confusion_pct: 72.0\n  result_rationale: '5-class F1 (0.62) is lower than 3-class (0.86) because deeper\n    classes (gen-2/gen-3/gen-4) have progressively overlapping feature distributions\n    — drift accumulates but with diminishing marginal signal per step. Gen-0 remains\n    easy (F1=0.92), gen-1 is moderate (F1=0.68), and gen-2/3/4 are difficult (F1≈0.48–0.52)\n    because the distributional differences between adjacent deep classes are small.\n    However, the high Spearman ρ (0.83) and low MAE (0.58) indicate that the classifier''s\n    errors are ordinal — when it misclassifies, it predicts an adjacent class (72%\n    of errors are off-by-one), not a distant one. This supports ordinal regression\n    as a natural formulation and suggests that while exact depth prediction is noisy\n    for deep classes, the relative ordering is preserved. For the paper, this result\n    supports the claim that drift is monotonic (H₂) while honestly reporting the practical\n    ceiling on depth resolution.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 13: 5-Class Ordinal Depth Classification'\n    columns:\n    - Class\n    - Precision\n    - Recall\n    - F1\n    rows:\n    - - gen-0\n      - 0.93\n      - 0.91\n      - 0.92\n    - - gen-1\n      - 0.66\n      - 0.7\n      - 0.68\n    - - gen-2\n      - 0.5\n      - 0.54\n      - 0.52\n    - - gen-3\n      - 0.47\n      - 0.49\n      - 0.48\n    - - gen-4\n      - 0.53\n      - 0.5\n      - 0.51\n    - - Macro\n      - —\n      - —\n      - 0.62\n  - name: 'Table 13b: Ordinal Error Analysis'\n    columns:\n    - Metric\n    - Value\n    rows:\n    - - MAE\n      - 0.58\n    - - Spearman ρ (pred vs true depth)\n      - 0.83\n    - - Adjacent-class confusion (%)\n      - 72%\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 9a: 5×5 confusion matrix heatmap. Diagonal + adjacent-diagonal cells are\n    dark, confirming ordinal error structure.'\n  - 'Fig 9b: Predicted depth (box plot) vs true depth. Boxes overlap for gen-2/3/4\n    but medians increase monotonically.'\n  analysis: 'The 5-class result characterizes the resolution ceiling of distributional\n    fingerprints. While exact depth-4 vs depth-3 discrimination is weak, the ordinal\n    structure is strong (ρ=0.83). This has practical implications: for data curation,\n    coarse depth buckets (gen-0 / gen-1 / gen-2+) are more useful than exact depth\n    anyway — the key decision boundary is \"how much drift is tolerable.\" The result\n    also differentiates our work from attractor theory: paraphrastic chains converge\n    to ~2 distinguishable classes, while our non-paraphrastic chains support at least\n    3 clean classes and 5 ordinal classes. This directly addresses RC3.\n\n    '\n- name: human_edit_robustness\n  plan_id: plan_010\n  description: 'Robustness test on plan_002 data with simulated edits: (A) random\n    word replacement at 5%, 10%, 20% rates, (B) LLM-simulated light editing (rephrase\n    random sentences). Tests depth signal persistence under post-generation editing.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    no_edit_macro_f1: 0.86\n    random_5pct_macro_f1: 0.83\n    random_10pct_macro_f1: 0.78\n    random_20pct_macro_f1: 0.67\n    llm_light_edit_macro_f1: 0.76\n    degradation_slope_per_10pct: -0.095\n  result_rationale: 'Random word replacement at 5% has minimal impact (F1=0.83, Δ=-0.03)\n    because replacing 5% of tokens introduces noise uniformly across all depth classes,\n    preserving the relative distributional differences. At 10% (F1=0.78), the noise\n    begins to mask depth-specific features — entropy statistics become noisier as\n    random words inject unpredictable surprise values. At 20% (F1=0.67), substantial\n    text corruption disrupts the distributional fingerprint significantly but performance\n    remains above chance (0.33). LLM-simulated editing (F1=0.76) is comparable to\n    10% random replacement because light rephrasing changes surface statistics but\n    largely preserves the underlying distributional structure — the edited text still\n    occupies the same region of reference-model feature space. The degradation is\n    approximately linear (-0.095 F1 per 10% edit rate), providing a simple model for\n    practitioners to assess signal reliability given estimated editing rates. These\n    results address RC5 while honestly reporting the method''s sensitivity to post-generation\n    modification.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 14: Robustness to Simulated Edits'\n    columns:\n    - Edit Type\n    - Edit Rate\n    - Macro-F1\n    - Δ from No-Edit\n    rows:\n    - - None (baseline)\n      - 0%\n      - 0.86\n      - —\n    - - Random word replacement\n      - 5%\n      - 0.83\n      - '-0.03'\n    - - Random word replacement\n      - 10%\n      - 0.78\n      - '-0.08'\n    - - Random word replacement\n      - 20%\n      - 0.67\n      - '-0.19'\n    - - LLM light editing\n      - ~8–12%\n      - 0.76\n      - '-0.10'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 10: Line plot — macro-F1 vs edit rate. Linear degradation with 95% CI band.\n    Horizontal dashed line at chance level (0.33). Annotation at 10% edit rate: ''typical\n    human editing range.'''\n  analysis: 'The robustness profile is realistic for practical deployment. Light editing\n    (5–10%) typical of human post-processing preserves most of the depth signal. Heavy\n    editing (20%) degrades performance significantly but remains above chance, suggesting\n    that even heavily edited text retains some distributional fingerprint. For the\n    paper, this experiment provides the necessary caveat about real-world applicability\n    while showing that the method is viable for the most common use case (lightly\n    edited or unedited synthetic text in web-scraped data).\n\n    '\n- name: ld_score_feature\n  plan_id: plan_011\n  description: 'Add LD-Score (character-distribution signature from arXiv:2605.01647)\n    as an additional feature to the plan_001 ablation framework. Tests whether character-level\n    features provide complementary depth signal beyond token-level distributional\n    features.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    full_plus_ld_macro_f1: 0.88\n    ld_score_only_macro_f1: 0.59\n    ld_score_permutation_importance: 0.06\n    full_plus_ld_vs_full_delta: 0.01\n  result_rationale: 'LD-Score alone achieves modest depth discrimination (F1=0.59)\n    because character-level distributional signatures capture some depth-dependent\n    patterns — deeper generation text has slightly more uniform character distributions\n    as vocabulary impoverishment affects character-level statistics. However, adding\n    LD-Score to the full feature set provides only marginal improvement (Δ=+0.01)\n    because LD-Score''s signal is largely redundant with existing surface features\n    (TTR, hapax_ratio) — both measure vocabulary impoverishment, just at different\n    granularities (character vs word level). The low permutation importance (0.06)\n    confirms that LD-Score is not providing new information. This is a useful negative-ish\n    result: it shows that character-level features do not add a fundamentally new\n    signal modality, reinforcing that the key complementary feature families are surface\n    vocabulary statistics and reference-model distributional features.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 15: LD-Score Feature Integration'\n    columns:\n    - Feature Set\n    - Macro-F1\n    - Δ from Full\n    rows:\n    - - Full (7 features)\n      - 0.87\n      - —\n    - - Full + LD-Score (8 features)\n      - 0.88\n      - '+0.01'\n    - - LD-Score only\n      - 0.59\n      - '-0.28'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig S1 (supplementary): Feature importance bar chart with LD-Score included.\n    LD-Score ranks below all reference-model features.'\n  analysis: 'A clean but unexciting result that belongs in the supplementary material.\n    It confirms the two-modality structure of depth signal (reference-model entropy\n    + surface vocabulary) and shows that adding more surface-level features (even\n    at a different granularity) does not expand the signal envelope. For the paper,\n    this addresses potential questions about \"what about other feature types\" and\n    justifies the focus on reference-model features as the novel contribution.\n\n    '\n- name: end_to_end_classifier\n  plan_id: plan_012\n  description: 'QLoRA fine-tune Qwen3.5-0.8B for end-to-end depth classification (directly\n    from text, no handcrafted features). Compares learned representations vs handcrafted\n    distributional features on the plan_002 dataset.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    qwen_qlora_macro_f1: 0.83\n    qwen_qlora_gen0_f1: 0.94\n    qwen_qlora_gen1_f1: 0.77\n    qwen_qlora_gen2plus_f1: 0.79\n    handcrafted_features_macro_f1: 0.86\n    qwen_qlora_cross_gen_leave_one_out_f1: 0.71\n    handcrafted_cross_gen_leave_one_out_f1: 0.78\n    qwen_qlora_training_gpu_hours: 12.0\n    handcrafted_training_time_minutes: 3.0\n  result_rationale: 'The QLoRA fine-tuned model achieves competitive in-distribution\n    performance (F1=0.83 vs handcrafted 0.86) because the transformer can implicitly\n    learn distributional features from raw text. However, it underperforms the handcrafted\n    approach for two reasons: (1) the handcrafted features distill the reference model''s\n    perspective into compact statistics that directly measure what matters (distributional\n    drift from human text), while the fine-tuned model must learn this implicitly\n    from text patterns; (2) the XGBoost classifier on 7 features is harder to overfit\n    than a 0.8B parameter model on ~25K training examples. The larger gap in cross-generator\n    leave-one-out (0.71 vs 0.78) reveals that the fine-tuned model memorizes generator-specific\n    artifacts more than the handcrafted features, which are designed to be generator-invariant.\n    The 4000× training time difference (12 GPU-hours vs 3 minutes) makes the handcrafted\n    approach clearly preferable for practical deployment. This result validates the\n    feature engineering approach over brute-force fine-tuning and justifies the paper''s\n    focus on interpretable distributional features.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 16: End-to-End vs Feature-Based Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - In-Dist F1\n    - Cross-Gen F1\n    - Training Cost\n    rows:\n    - - XGBoost + handcrafted features\n      - 0.86\n      - 0.78\n      - 3 min\n    - - QLoRA Qwen3.5-0.8B\n      - 0.83\n      - 0.71\n      - 12 GPU·h\n    - - Δ (handcrafted − QLoRA)\n      - '+0.03'\n      - '+0.07'\n      - —\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig S2 (supplementary): Paired bar chart — in-distribution vs cross-generator\n    F1 for both methods. Handcrafted features show smaller generalization gap.'\n  analysis: 'This is a strategically important result. Reviewers might ask \"why not\n    just fine-tune a model?\" — this experiment shows that handcrafted features outperform,\n    generalize better, are 4000× cheaper to train, and are interpretable (you can\n    point to specific features and explain why they work). The fine-tuned model''s\n    worse cross-generator generalization is the most telling result: it confirms that\n    the handcrafted features capture the right abstraction (distributional drift as\n    measured by a reference model) while the fine-tuned model takes shortcuts via\n    generator-specific patterns. This result belongs in the supplementary material\n    and reinforces the paper''s methodological choice.'\n","numLines":1095,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1095}}}
