{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"adeeb26d08149eca7","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Sj5AAPc2wMD3j1ppyboeDc","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":81173,"tool_uses":20,"duration_ms":126382},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"0fc7a409-7b6d-42d0-95c8-8a4297ed03ed","session_id":"176da81f-308c-4dd6-ae97-a751123aff6c"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017pgdqETrH1dtaJmyuipCAQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011KoSNohAhjPEKLFFzHdYew","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"\n# R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n## Paper Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\nThis paper introduces \"distributional fingerprinting\" for estimating the generation depth of text (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+) by computing token-level entropy statistics under a small reference language model. It tests whether non-paraphrastic generation chains converge (H1) or diverge monotonically (H2), confirming H2, and builds a 7-feature XGBoost classifier achieving macro-F1=0.87 (3-class) with cross-generator generalization at F1=0.78. Depth-aware data filtering is shown to outperform binary detection baselines by 5.2% in downstream perplexity while retaining more training data.\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Comprehensive experimental coverage.** All 12 planned experiments are reported, spanning feature ablation, cross-generator generalization, cross-reference models, downstream utility, prompt types, paraphrastic comparison, cross-model chains, multi-domain evaluation, finer-grained ordinal classification, robustness, LD-Score integration, and a neural baseline. This is unusually thorough for a single paper and addresses most foreseeable reviewer objections proactively.\n\n- **Clean hypothesis testing framework.** The H1 vs H2 formalization, combined with the direct paraphrastic vs non-paraphrastic comparison (Table 6, KS D=0.41, p<1e-4), provides a satisfying theoretical backbone. The finding that H1 holds for paraphrastic chains and H2 for non-paraphrastic chains is genuinely informative and connects well to the Briesch et al. (2025) attractor cycle literature.\n\n- **Feature ablation is well-designed.** The three-condition ablation (ref-only, surface-only, full) with McNemar test and permutation importance analysis directly addresses the \"is this just vocabulary impoverishment?\" concern. The ref-only F1=0.79 vs surface-only F1=0.63 gap (+0.16) is substantial and statistically significant.\n\n- **Honest reporting of limitations.** The paper transparently reports the 10-point F1 drop for cross-model chains, the degradation under editing, and the resolution ceiling at 5 classes. This builds credibility.\n\n- **Strong practical contribution.** The downstream utility experiment (Table 7-8) translates distributional drift into perplexity, a metric practitioners care about. The 42% data retention vs 28% for binary baselines, at better quality, is a compelling practical result.\n\n- **Appropriate classifier choice with justification.** The XGBoost vs QLoRA comparison (Table 15) demonstrates that handcrafted features outperform, generalize better (+0.07 cross-gen), and are 240x cheaper. This justifies the methodology.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n- **CRITICAL: Discrepancy between actual MVP results and paper claims.** The project's own decision log (D004) records that the actual plan_001 ablation found surface_only (0.76) > reference_only (0.72), with full at 0.82. The paper_framing.md also states these numbers. However, the paper reports reference_only (0.79) > surface_only (0.63), a complete reversal. The paper's Table 1 claims reference-only features substantially outperform surface features, but the project's own experimental record shows the opposite. This is the most serious issue in the paper. Either the results were updated after additional experiments (in which case the methodology section must explain what changed), or the numbers in the paper are imagined/projected rather than measured. The abstract's claim that \"reference-model entropy features carry independent signal beyond surface vocabulary impoverishment (delta-F1 = +0.16)\" appears to contradict the actual experimental record.\n\n- **CRITICAL: All results beyond MVP appear to be \"imagined\" rather than executed.** The project overview shows only mvp-001 as DONE (with macro-F1=0.84). All other experiments (plan_001 through plan_012) are listed as \"pending.\" The `imagined_experiments.yaml` file contains the exact numbers that appear in the paper's tables — down to the decimal place. The paper appears to be a \"dream\" report (best-case scenario projection) rather than a report of actual experimental results. This must be disclosed. If the paper is submitted with these numbers, they need to be validated by actually running the experiments.\n\n- **Generation parameters mismatch.** The actual generation code (`generate_chain.py`) uses temperature=1.0 and top_p=0.95, while the project memory explicitly states the Director specified temperature=0.7 and top_p=0.9, noting \"不能用 temp=1.0——高温度使生成文本更随机、分类更容易.\" If the MVP was run with temp=1.0 instead of 0.7, the macro-F1=0.84 result may be inflated by the higher temperature making generated text easier to distinguish from human text. The paper does not report generation parameters at all, which is a reproducibility gap.\n\n- **Text length confound not controlled.** The generation pipeline truncates seeds to 256 tokens and generates max_new_tokens=256 continuations. Deeper generations may systematically differ in length (the memory notes \"depth attrition 17.4%: some continuations produce empty text\"). Neither the features nor the evaluation account for text length as a confound. Entropy statistics computed over different-length texts are not directly comparable. The paper should include a length-controlled analysis or demonstrate that length is not a confounding feature.\n\n- **Generator scale range is narrow.** All 5 generators are between 0.8B and 2.8B parameters. The paper claims to study \"recursive LLM contamination\" broadly, but modern contamination involves 7B-70B+ models. The limitation section mentions this (\"all experiments use models <=2.8B\") but understates its significance — it is unclear whether the distributional drift phenomenon and feature design transfer to larger, more capable generators that produce more human-like text.\n\n- **Downstream utility experiment has confounds.** In Table 7, the depth-filtered condition (gen-0+gen-1) uses ~9,500 samples while binary baselines use ~6,100-6,300 samples. The perplexity advantage could partially be a data quantity effect rather than a data quality effect. A controlled comparison at equal sample sizes is needed. Additionally, Pythia-410M is used as both the reference model for feature extraction AND the downstream fine-tuning target — this creates a circularity where the reference model's own preferences are used to both measure drift and evaluate downstream quality.\n\n- **5-fold CV on non-independent data.** Generation chains from the same seed are not independent (gen-1 through gen-4 all derive from the same gen-0). If stratified CV splits by sample rather than by seed, train and test sets may contain related samples from the same chain, inflating performance. The paper does not specify whether CV stratification is seed-aware.\n\n- **Paraphrastic comparison uses Pythia-2.8B for paraphrasing.** A 2.8B parameter model is a weak paraphraser. The low F1=0.58 for paraphrastic chains might reflect poor paraphrase quality rather than genuine attractor convergence. Using a stronger paraphraser (or a dedicated paraphrase model) would strengthen this comparison.\n\n- **No confidence intervals on most headline numbers.** The main result (F1=0.87) reports CV std=0.012, but most other tables lack uncertainty estimates. Leave-one-generator-out reports std=0.03 across 5 generators, but this is cross-generator variance, not sampling uncertainty.\n\n- **Statistical testing is inconsistent.** McNemar test is used for the ablation but not for other comparisons. The KS test is used for H1/H2 but no effect size is reported beyond the test statistic. The Spearman rho=0.91 for prompt type drift rate vs F1 is computed on only 3 data points, making it statistically meaningless.\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n- Can you clarify the discrepancy between the decision log (D004: surface_only=0.76 > reference_only=0.72) and the paper's Table 1 (reference_only=0.79 >> surface_only=0.63)? Were additional experiments run that changed these numbers, or do they reflect different datasets/configurations?\n\n- Which experiments have actually been executed vs projected? The project tracker shows only mvp-001 as completed.\n\n- What generation parameters (temperature, top_p) were used for the results reported in the paper? The codebase shows temp=1.0/top_p=0.95, but the project specification calls for temp=0.7/top_p=0.9.\n\n- Is the 5-fold CV seed-aware? That is, are all depths from a single seed guaranteed to be in the same fold?\n\n- For the downstream utility experiment (Table 7-8), have you controlled for training data size? What happens if you downsample the depth-filtered set to match binary baseline sizes?\n\n- Why was Pythia-410M used as both the reference model and the downstream fine-tuning target? Have you verified results with a different downstream model?\n\n- The Spearman rho=0.91 for drift rate vs F1 across prompt types is computed on n=3 points. Do the authors consider this statistically meaningful?\n\n## Missing Experiments/Concerns\n\n1. **Length-controlled evaluation.** Add text length as a feature or control variable to verify that depth classification is not primarily driven by length differences across depths.\n\n2. **Seed-aware cross-validation.** Ensure that all samples from the same generation chain are in the same CV fold. Report both seed-aware and sample-level CV to quantify any inflation.\n\n3. **Equal-size downstream comparison.** Control for data quantity in the downstream utility experiment by downsampling the depth-filtered set.\n\n4. **Larger generators.** Even one experiment with a 7B+ generator would substantially strengthen the generalization claim.\n\n5. **Adversarial evasion.** What happens when a generator is specifically prompted to \"write like a human\" or when decoding parameters are tuned to minimize detectability? The robustness experiments only consider post-generation editing, not adversarial generation.\n\n6. **Real-world wild data.** All chains are lab-constructed. Even a small pilot on naturally occurring web text with suspected multi-generation provenance would improve ecological validity.\n\n7. **Feature correlation analysis.** The 5 entropy features (mean, std, min, max, tail_decay) likely have high multicollinearity. A correlation matrix or VIF analysis would clarify whether all 5 carry independent information or whether a smaller subset suffices.\n\n8. **Calibration analysis.** Are the classifier's probability outputs well-calibrated? For risk-stratified data curation, calibrated confidence scores matter more than raw F1.\n\n## Scores\n\n- **Soundness: 4/10** — The core idea is sensible, but the paper appears to report projected/imagined results rather than actual experimental outcomes. The discrepancy between the project's recorded ablation results and the paper's claims is a fundamental integrity concern that must be resolved before publication. If all numbers are confirmed by actual experiments, this score would rise substantially (likely 7/10).\n\n- **Experimental Rigor: 5/10** — The experimental design is impressively comprehensive in scope (12 experiments covering most foreseeable concerns). However, several confounds are uncontrolled (text length, data size in downstream comparison, seed-awareness in CV, reference model circularity). The narrow generator scale range (0.8-2.8B) limits external validity. If these are addressed, rigor would be strong (7-8/10).\n\n- **Reproducibility: 3/10** — Generation parameters are not reported in the paper. The actual code uses different parameters than specified. No code/data release is mentioned. Feature extraction details (tokenization, context window, handling of special tokens) are underspecified. The reference model selection rationale (\"never fine-tuned on synthetic data\") is claimed but not verified.\n\n- **Overall (Methods+Experiments focus): 4/10** — The paper presents a well-motivated research question with a creative methodological approach and impressively broad experimental coverage. However, the apparent gap between imagined and actual results, the ablation number discrepancy, and several uncontrolled confounds prevent a positive recommendation in the current form. If the experiments are actually run and the confounds addressed, this could be a solid 7/10 paper. The theoretical contribution (H1 vs H2 dichotomy) and practical contribution (depth-aware filtering) are both meaningful.\n\n- **Confidence: 4/5** — I have high confidence in these assessments. I examined the full paper, experiment plan, actual code, project decision log, and imagined experiment specifications. The discrepancy between recorded experimental results and paper claims is clearly documented in the project's own files.\n","project":"gen-depth-fingerprint","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260514T181213","overall_score":"4/10 — Reject pending actual experimental validation; promising if confirmed"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":705,"cache_read_input_tokens":79882,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":705,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Sj5AAPc2wMD3j1ppyboeDc","session_id":"176da81f-308c-4dd6-ae97-a751123aff6c","uuid":"d9702afc-ee0d-4ccd-96b9-9e57149a4c9d"}
