{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Sj5AAPc2wMD3j1ppyboeDc","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Overall Assessment: 4/10 (Reject pending experimental validation)**\n\nThe paper presents an interesting and well-motivated research direction -- distributional fingerprinting for generation depth estimation -- with impressively comprehensive experimental design (12 experiments). However, I identified several critical issues:\n\n**Most serious findings:**\n\n1. **Results appear to be projections, not actuals.** The project tracker shows only `mvp-001` as completed. All 12 paper experiments match the `imagined_experiments.yaml` file to the decimal place, suggesting the paper reports best-case projections rather than executed experiments.\n\n2. **Ablation numbers contradict the project's own records.** Decision D004 records that the actual ablation found surface_only (0.76) > reference_only (0.72), but the paper claims reference_only (0.79) >> surface_only (0.63) -- a complete reversal that undermines the core claim about reference-model features.\n\n3. **Generation parameter mismatch.** The code uses temperature=1.0/top_p=0.95, while the Director specified 0.7/0.9, noting that temp=1.0 inflates classification results.\n\n**Additional methodological concerns:** text length confound, non-seed-aware CV, data size confound in downstream experiments, reference model circularity (Pythia-410M used for both feature extraction and downstream evaluation), narrow generator scale (0.8-2.8B only), and a Spearman correlation computed on n=3 points.\n\n**If the experiments are actually run and confounds addressed**, the paper could reach 7/10 -- the theoretical framework (H1/H2 dichotomy), experimental breadth, and practical application (depth-aware filtering) are all well-conceived.\n\nReview saved to: `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_3/projects/gen-depth-fingerprint/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T181213/best/review_report.md`"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: adeeb26d08149eca7 (use SendMessage with to: 'adeeb26d08149eca7' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 84393\ntool_uses: 20\nduration_ms: 141425</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"176da81f-308c-4dd6-ae97-a751123aff6c","uuid":"32eb30f9-6fb8-4e81-ac44-d214e85cd899","timestamp":"2026-05-14T18:45:38.859Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind peer review of an academic paper submitted to AAAI 2027. Your focus areas are **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## AAAI Review Criteria\nAAAI uses a 1-10 overall assessment scale. Acceptance rate ~23%. Dimensions: soundness, significance, novelty, clarity, relevance. The review form asks for a paper summary, strengths, weaknesses, questions for authors, and an overall score.\n\nScale:\n- 10: Top 5%, seminal\n- 9: Strong accept\n- 8: Clear accept\n- 7: Accept, good paper with minor weaknesses\n- 6: Weak accept / borderline\n- 5: Borderline / weak reject\n- 4: Reject, below threshold\n- 3: Clear reject\n- 2: Strong reject\n- 1: Trivial or wrong\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Distributional Fingerprints of Generation Depth: Detecting Recursive LLM Contamination Beyond Binary Classification\"\n\nThe paper introduces \"distributional fingerprinting\" for estimating the generation depth of text (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+) by measuring distributional divergence from human text under a small reference LM. It tests two hypotheses about non-paraphrastic generation chains: convergence (H1) vs monotonic divergence (H2), confirming H2. A 7-feature classifier (5 reference-model entropy features + 2 surface features) with XGBoost achieves macro-F1=0.87 (3-class) and generalizes across 5 generators (leave-one-out F1=0.78). Depth-aware data filtering retains 42% data at near-human perplexity, outperforming binary baselines by 5.2%.\n\n## Key Results (from paper)\n- Table 1: Feature ablation — ref-only F1=0.79, surface-only F1=0.63, full F1=0.87, delta=+0.16\n- Table 2: Permutation importance — entropy_mean=0.28 (top), hapax_ratio drops from 0.30 XGB gain to 0.14 permutation\n- Table 3: Leave-one-generator-out — mean F1=0.78±0.03 across 5 generators\n- Table 4: Per-generator in-distribution — pooled F1=0.86\n- Table 5: Cross-reference models — Pythia-410M F1=0.86, OLMo-2-1B F1=0.76, Qwen3.5-0.8B F1=0.74, ensemble F1=0.88\n- Table 6: Paraphrastic vs continuation — continuation F1=0.85, paraphrastic F1=0.58, KS D=0.41 p<1e-4\n- Table 7-8: Downstream utility — depth-filtered PPL=29.0, binary baselines PPL=31.8-32.1, human PPL=28.3\n- Table 9: Prompt types — continuation F1=0.85, elaboration F1=0.80, inspired_rewriting F1=0.72, drift rate correlation ρ=0.91\n- Table 10: Cross-model chains — F1=0.75, 10-point drop from single-model\n- Table 11: Multi-domain — Wikipedia F1=0.86, News F1=0.83, Scientific F1=0.81, Creative F1=0.78, leave-one-out F1=0.77\n- Table 12: 5-class ordinal — macro-F1=0.62, Spearman ρ=0.83, MAE=0.58\n- Table 13: Robustness — 5% edit F1=0.83, 10% F1=0.78, 20% F1=0.67\n- Table 14: LD-Score integration — marginal +0.01\n- Table 15: Neural baseline — XGBoost F1=0.86/0.78 (in/cross), QLoRA F1=0.83/0.71, XGBoost 3min vs 12 GPU·h\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (what was planned)\nThe full experiment plan includes 12 experiments:\n1. feature_ablation (plan_001) — covered in paper\n2. cross_generator_evaluation (plan_002) — covered\n3. cross_family_reference_model (plan_003) — covered\n4. downstream_utility (plan_004) — covered\n5. prompt_type_comparison (plan_005) — covered\n6. paraphrastic_comparison (plan_006) — covered\n7. cross_model_chains (plan_007) — covered\n8. multi_domain_seeds (plan_008) — covered\n9. finer_grained_ordinal (plan_009) — covered\n10. human_edit_robustness (plan_010) — covered\n11. ld_score_feature (plan_011) — covered\n12. end_to_end_classifier (plan_012) — covered\n\nAll 12 planned experiments appear in the paper.\n\n## Your Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness**: Are the methods technically correct? Are the experimental designs valid? Are there confounds or methodological issues?\n2. **Experimental Rigor**: Are the experiments comprehensive? Are baselines appropriate? Are statistical tests used correctly? Is there proper cross-validation?\n3. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce the results?\n4. **Specific Technical Concerns**: Any issues with feature design, classifier choice, evaluation metrics, etc.\n5. **Missing Experiments or Baselines**: What's not covered that should be?\n\nFormat your review as:\n\n### Paper Summary (2-3 sentences)\n### Strengths (bulleted)\n### Weaknesses (bulleted)\n### Questions for Authors (bulleted)\n### Missing Experiments/Concerns\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/10\n- Experimental Rigor: X/10\n- Reproducibility: X/10\n- Overall (Methods+Experiments focus): X/10\n- Confidence: X/5\n","agentId":"adeeb26d08149eca7","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Overall Assessment: 4/10 (Reject pending experimental validation)**\n\nThe paper presents an interesting and well-motivated research direction -- distributional fingerprinting for generation depth estimation -- with impressively comprehensive experimental design (12 experiments). However, I identified several critical issues:\n\n**Most serious findings:**\n\n1. **Results appear to be projections, not actuals.** The project tracker shows only `mvp-001` as completed. All 12 paper experiments match the `imagined_experiments.yaml` file to the decimal place, suggesting the paper reports best-case projections rather than executed experiments.\n\n2. **Ablation numbers contradict the project's own records.** Decision D004 records that the actual ablation found surface_only (0.76) > reference_only (0.72), but the paper claims reference_only (0.79) >> surface_only (0.63) -- a complete reversal that undermines the core claim about reference-model features.\n\n3. **Generation parameter mismatch.** The code uses temperature=1.0/top_p=0.95, while the Director specified 0.7/0.9, noting that temp=1.0 inflates classification results.\n\n**Additional methodological concerns:** text length confound, non-seed-aware CV, data size confound in downstream experiments, reference model circularity (Pythia-410M used for both feature extraction and downstream evaluation), narrow generator scale (0.8-2.8B only), and a Spearman correlation computed on n=3 points.\n\n**If the experiments are actually run and confounds addressed**, the paper could reach 7/10 -- the theoretical framework (H1/H2 dichotomy), experimental breadth, and practical application (depth-aware filtering) are all well-conceived.\n\nReview saved to: `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_3/projects/gen-depth-fingerprint/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T181213/best/review_report.md`"}],"totalDurationMs":141425,"totalTokens":84393,"totalToolUseCount":20,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3332,"cache_read_input_tokens":80587,"output_tokens":473,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3332},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":473,"cache_read_input_tokens":80587,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3332,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3332,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":7,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a2d15da6165e8b28b","tool_use_id":"toolu_01LifoSGx9oPwZY1zX1HTfDm","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":95075,"tool_uses":19,"duration_ms":137072},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"d15ea933-89b5-4a5b-9757-ae550118b7b5","session_id":"176da81f-308c-4dd6-ae97-a751123aff6c"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SmrxK4nRke55FE83cv7wwU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NFRGaxhbaEbQRXhqo2cdvs","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"gen-depth-fingerprint","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260514T181213","overall_score":"5 - Borderline / Weak Reject","report":"# Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty and Related Work\n\n## Paper Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\nThis paper introduces \"distributional fingerprinting\" for estimating the generation depth of text (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+) by computing token-level entropy statistics under a small reference language model. The method uses a 7-feature XGBoost classifier (5 reference-model entropy features + 2 surface vocabulary features) and tests two hypotheses about non-paraphrastic generation chains, confirming monotonic divergence (H2). The approach achieves macro-F1=0.87 (3-class) with cross-generator generalization (leave-one-out F1=0.78), and demonstrates downstream utility via depth-aware data filtering.\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Novel problem formulation.** Framing generation depth estimation as a graded, ordinal classification task (rather than binary real/synthetic detection) is a genuinely useful reframing with clear practical motivation for data curation pipelines. The paper correctly identifies a real gap: binary detectors force a lossy all-or-nothing decision. This problem formulation alone has value for the community.\n\n- **Clean theoretical framing via H1/H2 dichotomy.** The contrast between convergent paraphrastic chains (H1, connecting to Briesch et al. 2025 attractor cycle theory) and divergent non-paraphrastic chains (H2) provides a satisfying conceptual structure. The direct empirical comparison (Table 5 / Section 4.4) with KS statistics is well-executed and produces a clear result. The insight that the presence or absence of a semantic-preservation constraint determines the dynamics regime is theoretically interesting.\n\n- **Comprehensive experimental coverage.** The paper tests across 5 generators, 4 domains, 3 prompt types, cross-model chains, cross-reference models, robustness to editing, and finer-grained ordinal classification. This breadth is commendable and addresses many obvious reviewer concerns preemptively.\n\n- **Honest reporting of limitations.** The cross-model chain degradation (-10 F1), the editing robustness degradation curve, and the 5-class resolution ceiling are transparently reported. This increases credibility.\n\n- **Practical interpretability.** The 7-feature XGBoost approach is lightweight (3 minutes training), interpretable, and outperforms the QLoRA neural baseline on cross-generator transfer. This is a practical strength for real-world deployment.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### W1: The \"distributional fingerprint\" is largely perplexity-based OOD detection repackaged (Major)\n\nThe 5 reference-model features (mean, std, min, max of token-level NLL + tail decay) are essentially summary statistics of the text's perplexity profile under a reference model. This is functionally equivalent to well-established out-of-distribution detection via likelihood-based statistics. The paper does not cite or differentiate from:\n\n- **DetectGPT (Mitchell et al. 2023)**: Uses perturbation-based log-probability curvature under a reference model -- the same conceptual apparatus of \"measuring distributional displacement from a reference model's expectations.\" The \"best\" paper cites FastDetectGPT but never discusses DetectGPT itself or explains how the entropy features differ from log-probability curvature.\n- **DNA-GPT (Yang et al. 2023)**: Uses divergent N-gram analysis for machine text detection, directly relevant to measuring distributional divergence.\n- **Perplexity-based detection** more broadly (Solaiman et al. 2019; Gehrmann et al. 2019 GLTR): These foundational works established that perplexity under a reference model distinguishes human from machine text. The entropy features in this paper are direct descendants of this idea, extended to the ordinal depth setting. The paper should acknowledge this lineage and articulate what is genuinely new beyond applying known perplexity-based features to a new (depth) classification target.\n\nThe novelty of the features themselves is limited. What is new is the *application* to depth estimation rather than binary detection. The paper should be more forthright about this distinction.\n\n### W2: Critically thin related work -- only 5 references (Major)\n\nFor a venue like AAAI, a related work section with only 5 references is unacceptable. Entire subfields that are directly relevant are omitted:\n\n**Missing: Data contamination and benchmark contamination.**\n- Sainz et al. (2023), \"NLP Evaluation in Trouble\": benchmark contamination from LLM outputs\n- Oren et al. (2024), \"Proving Test Set Contamination in Black Box Language Models\"\n- Golchin & Surdeanu (2024), \"Time Travel in LLMs: Tracing Data Contamination in Large Language Models\"\n- The entire line on data decontamination in LLM training pipelines\n\n**Missing: Membership inference and training data attribution.**\n- Shi et al. (2024), \"Detecting Pretraining Data from Large Language Models\" (Min-K% Prob)\n- Carlini et al. (2021/2023) on extracting training data and memorization\n- These methods are conceptually adjacent: they also use likelihood-based statistics under a reference model to infer provenance\n\n**Missing: Watermarking beyond SynthID.**\n- Kirchenbauer et al. (2023), \"A Watermark for Large Language Models\" -- the foundational LLM watermarking paper\n- Christ et al. (2024), Zhao et al. (2024) -- undetectable watermarks\n- Watermarking is a competing paradigm for tracking generation provenance. Omitting it entirely is a gap.\n\n**Missing: Model collapse theory beyond Shumailov et al.**\n- Dohmatob et al. (2024), \"A Tale of Tails\" -- theoretical analysis of model collapse as tail distribution loss. Directly relevant to tail_decay feature.\n- Alemohammad et al. (2024), \"Self-Consuming Generative Models Go MAD\" -- recursive generation dynamics\n- Gerstgrasser et al. (2024) -- model collapse with data mixing\n\n**Missing: Perplexity-based and likelihood-based detection.**\n- Mitchell et al. (2023), DetectGPT\n- Solaiman et al. (2019), \"Release Strategies and Social Impacts of Language Models\" -- original GPT-2 output detection via likelihood\n- Gehrmann et al. (2019), GLTR -- statistical detection using token-level likelihood\n- Su et al. (2023), \"DetectLLM\"\n- Bao et al. (2024) is cited but the broader perplexity-detection lineage is not\n\n**Missing: Data quality scoring and data selection.**\n- Xie et al. (2023), \"Data Selection with DSIR\" -- distributional shift for data curation\n- Wettig et al. (2024), \"QuRating\" -- quality-based data selection\n- The downstream utility claim (Claim 3) directly competes with this literature. Depth-aware filtering should be compared against quality scoring methods, not just binary detectors.\n\n**Missing: Distributional shift and OOD detection.**\n- The entropy features are functionally OOD detection features. The connection to OOD detection (Hendrycks & Gimpel 2017; Liang et al. 2018 ODIN; Liu et al. 2020 energy-based) should be discussed. The paper's core technical contribution -- measuring distributional displacement from a reference model -- is standard in this literature.\n\n### W3: Novelty of the method is incremental (Moderate)\n\nThe technical method consists of: (1) compute NLL statistics under a reference LM, (2) add TTR and hapax ratio, (3) train XGBoost. Each component is well-established. The reference-model features are textbook perplexity statistics. The surface features (TTR, hapax) are standard lexical diversity measures used since the 1940s. XGBoost is off-the-shelf. The novelty lies in the problem formulation (depth estimation) and the empirical finding (H2 for non-paraphrastic chains), not in any methodological innovation. This is not necessarily fatal, but the paper oversells the method as a \"distributional fingerprinting\" technique when it is more accurately described as \"perplexity profiling applied to depth estimation.\"\n\n### W4: Ablation results are internally inconsistent across paper versions (Moderate)\n\nComparing the \"best\" paper's ablation (ref-only=0.79 >> surface-only=0.63) against the actual MVP results in memory.md (hapax_ratio importance=0.30, top feature; macro-F1=0.84), there is a large discrepancy. The MVP actually showed surface features dominating (hapax_ratio=0.30 importance), and the paper_framing.md acknowledges the framing had to pivot from \"independence\" to \"complementarity\" after the ablation showed surface features were strong. The \"best\" paper presents an optimistic scenario where reference features dominate, but the actual MVP data showed the opposite pattern. The paper's narrative that reference-model entropy features are the \"novel signal source\" carrying \"independent signal beyond surface vocabulary impoverishment\" needs to survive the actual ablation; the MVP data suggests surface features may be stronger than the best-case paper implies.\n\n### W5: The downstream utility comparison is not apples-to-apples (Minor)\n\nThe depth-aware filter keeps gen-0+gen-1 (42% data), while the binary baselines retain 28% data. The comparison conflates two effects: (a) depth-awareness and (b) more training data. A fairer comparison would match data retention rates. At matched 42% retention, how does a binary detector's top-42%-confidence subset compare? Without this control, the 5.2% improvement claim is confounded by data quantity.\n\n### W6: All generators are small (<3B parameters) (Minor)\n\nThe paper acknowledges this in limitations but the absence of any frontier-scale model (7B+, let alone 70B+) is a significant gap for practical claims about data curation. Larger models produce text that is much closer to human text distributions; the depth signal may vanish or change qualitatively. The claim \"distributional fingerprints enable risk-stratified data curation\" is overstated without evidence at the scales where data curation actually matters.\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n- How do you distinguish your reference-model entropy features from standard perplexity-based detection features (DetectGPT, GLTR)? Is the novelty purely in applying these features to depth estimation rather than binary detection?\n\n- Have you tested whether simply using raw perplexity (mean NLL) as a single feature achieves comparable depth classification? Given that entropy_mean is your top feature by permutation importance, what is the marginal value of the other 4 reference-model features beyond mean perplexity?\n\n- The comparison against binary baselines (FastDetectGPT, LD-Score) in the downstream utility experiment uses the baselines' default thresholds. Were these thresholds optimized for the data curation task, or are they generic detection thresholds? An unfair baseline comparison would undermine Claim 3.\n\n- What happens with instruction-tuned or RLHF-aligned generators? These are the models most commonly deployed in practice and may have very different distributional properties than base models like Pythia.\n\n- The 68% cycle detection rate in paraphrastic chains is interesting. Does this metric exist for continuation chains? If continuation chains show 0% cycle detection at all depths, that would strongly support H2; if there is some periodicity at deeper depths, H2 may be more nuanced than presented.\n\n- Can you provide a theoretical argument (not just empirical observation) for why non-paraphrastic chains should diverge monotonically? The paper gestures at \"unbounded semantic state space\" but does not formalize this.\n\n## Missing Related Work\n\nThe paper cites only 5 references. A minimally adequate related work section for AAAI should cite approximately 25-40 references. The following areas need coverage:\n\n1. **Perplexity-based detection lineage**: DetectGPT, GLTR, Solaiman et al. 2019, Su et al. 2023 DetectLLM\n2. **Data contamination detection**: Sainz et al. 2023, Oren et al. 2024, Golchin & Surdeanu 2024\n3. **Membership inference / training data attribution**: Shi et al. 2024 Min-K%, Carlini et al. 2021/2023\n4. **LLM watermarking**: Kirchenbauer et al. 2023, Christ et al. 2024, Zhao et al. 2024\n5. **Model collapse theory**: Dohmatob et al. 2024, Alemohammad et al. 2024, Gerstgrasser et al. 2024\n6. **Data quality/selection for LLM training**: Xie et al. 2023 DSIR, Wettig et al. 2024 QuRating, Penedo et al. 2024 FineWeb\n7. **OOD detection** (the technical parent of the reference-model features): Hendrycks & Gimpel 2017, energy-based OOD\n8. **Iterative self-improvement / self-consuming models**: Huang et al. 2023, Alemohammad et al. 2024\n\n## Scores\n\n- **Novelty: 5/10** -- The problem formulation (depth estimation) is new and well-motivated. The H1/H2 framing adds theoretical structure. However, the method itself (perplexity statistics + lexical diversity + XGBoost) is not novel. The \"distributional fingerprinting\" framing overclaims the methodological contribution.\n\n- **Related Work: 3/10** -- Five references is critically inadequate for AAAI. Multiple directly relevant subfields (perplexity-based detection, data contamination, membership inference, watermarking, OOD detection, data quality scoring, model collapse theory beyond one paper) are entirely absent. The paper fails to position itself within the broader landscape.\n\n- **Significance: 6/10** -- The problem matters. Depth estimation is a useful framing for data governance. The H2 confirmation for non-paraphrastic chains is a genuine empirical contribution. The practical utility (depth-aware filtering) is modest but real. However, the limited model scale and the downstream comparison confounds temper significance.\n\n- **Clarity: 7/10** -- Well-structured with comprehensive experiments. The H1/H2 framing is clear. Tables are informative. The writing is direct. However, the paper would benefit from acknowledging its methodological simplicity rather than dressing it up as \"distributional fingerprinting.\"\n\n- **Overall (Novelty+Related Work focus): 5/10** -- The problem formulation and empirical findings are valuable, but the paper is not ready for AAAI in its current form. The related work is unacceptably thin. The methodological novelty is incremental. The framing overclaims relative to what is actually new (applying known perplexity features to a new classification target). With a substantially expanded related work section, honest positioning of methodological novelty, and results at larger model scales, this could become a solid paper.\n\n- **Confidence: 4/5** -- I am familiar with the synthetic text detection, OOD detection, and model collapse literatures. I am confident in my assessment of the missing related work and the novelty calibration. I have some uncertainty about whether the best-case experimental results would hold in practice, given the discrepancy between the MVP data and the paper's optimistic ablation narrative.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":805,"cache_read_input_tokens":93645,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":805,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LifoSGx9oPwZY1zX1HTfDm","session_id":"176da81f-308c-4dd6-ae97-a751123aff6c","uuid":"30a7b652-5317-4a8b-aa1b-27ca55e72ec9"}
